window.QTT={"hub":{"title":"Queensland Time Travel","series":"A Frontier Friction series companion","seriesLine":"This app is a companion to the Frontier Friction series by Robin McKean: Shipwrecked on Ancient Ground, Troubled Paths on Ancient Ground and Resettled on Ancient Ground. All three books are coming soon.","kicker":"Time Travel on Country","subtitle":"Four eras of Queensland history. Visit each one, make the choices, and then see what really happened.","siteHome":"https://robmac10.github.io/history.html","hero":{"era":"gold","image":"gympie"},"intro":["In this app you travel back in time to four eras of Queensland history. The app does not explain how you travel.","In each era you take the place of a person who lived then. You see what they see and you make their choices. The people are invented. You cannot change history.","When the story ends you come back to the present. A page called “What really happened” shows the real history and links to the information bank for that era."],"steps":[{"n":"1","h":"Travel","p":"Choose an era. You arrive at that time and place."},{"n":"2","h":"Choose","p":"You take the place of an invented person and make their choices. Each choice has a cost."},{"n":"3","h":"Return","p":"Your visit ends and you are back in the present. Read what really happened."}],"bookLine":"This app goes with the book Walking on Ancient Ground (Time Travel on Country, A Choose Your Own Character Adventure) by Robin McKean.","warning":["Cultural content warning. This app may contain names and images of deceased Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander and South Sea Islander people.","It is about frontier violence, racism, forced removal and unfair labour in Queensland. It does not describe violence in detail, but it does say that it happened. You can stop at any time. If it upsets you, talk to a teacher, a family member or someone you trust."],"review":"Review status: this app has not yet been reviewed by Traditional Owners or by Australian South Sea Islander community groups. The author is not a member of these communities. Teachers, please seek advice from the Traditional Owners and community groups of your area before you use it in class.","returnText":"Your visit is over. You are back in the present. Nothing you chose changed history. Below is what really happened.","order":["grass","np","gold","sugar"],"eras":{"grass":{"name":"The Grass Rush","short":"Grass","span":[1840,1902],"spanLabel":"1840 to 1902","place":"The Darling Downs and central Queensland","visitLabel":"Starts in 1840, 1846 or 1869","blurb":"Squatters take up the grassland of the Darling Downs for sheep. Over sixty years, who holds the grass, which animals eat it and who has a say all change.","who":["Will, a shepherd (1846)","Alex, a station manager (1869)","An unnamed Aboriginal person (1840)"],"color":"#b9cf5c","deep":"#3b4a1c","hero":"yandilla","arrival":["You travel back to the Darling Downs, in what is now Queensland. Your visit can start in 1840, 1846 or 1869. It depends on whose place you take.","There are three invented people to choose from. The Aboriginal person has no name and no family group on purpose. You can come back and visit again as someone else."],"arrivalReal":[["w2","real",0],["w2","real",1]],"countryReal":[["w2",1]]},"np":{"name":"The Native Police","short":"Native Police","span":[1849,1904],"spanLabel":"1849 to 1904","place":"Central Queensland","visitLabel":"Set in the late 1870s","blurb":"A force of Aboriginal men, led by white officers, is used to “disperse” Aboriginal people from land that settlers want. You play a young trooper who has to decide what to report.","who":["Billy, a young trooper"],"color":"#ee8a5f","deep":"#5a2414","hero":"quay","arrival":["You travel back to Central Queensland in the late 1870s. You take the place of Billy, an invented trooper of about seventeen in the Native Mounted Police.","The Native Mounted Police was a force of Aboriginal men who were led by white officers. The story does not describe violence in detail, but the real history panels say plainly what the records show."],"arrivalReal":[["camp","real",1],["briefing","real",0]],"begin":"how","countryReal":[["ride",1]]},"gold":{"name":"The Gold Rush","short":"Gold","span":[1858,1877],"spanLabel":"1858 to 1877","place":"Canoona, Gympie, Charters Towers and the Palmer River","visitLabel":"Starts in 1858, 1873 or 1874","blurb":"Gold is found again and again between 1858 and 1877. Thousands of people rush to each field, and every field is on Aboriginal Country.","who":["Sam, a labourer (1858)","Lin, a Chinese miner (1874)","An unnamed Aboriginal person (1873)"],"color":"#f4bc3a","deep":"#5a4208","hero":"gympie","arrival":["You travel back to Queensland in the years of the gold rushes. Your visit can start in 1858, 1873 or 1874. It depends on whose place you take.","There are three invented people to choose from. Each one lives through a different part of the gold rushes. The Aboriginal person has no name and no family group on purpose."],"arrivalReal":[["s2","real",0],["a1","real",0]],"countryReal":[["a1",0]]},"sugar":{"name":"The Sugar Story","short":"Sugar","span":[1863,1908],"spanLabel":"1863 to 1908","place":"The Mackay district","visitLabel":"Starts in 1884","blurb":"Ships recruit young Pacific Islanders to work in Queensland’s sugar cane. Follow Harry from 1884 to the law of 1901 and the deportations of 1906 to 1908.","who":["Harry, a young man from the New Hebrides"],"color":"#55cdb0","deep":"#0f4a40","hero":"mill","arrival":["You travel back to 1884. You take the place of Harry, an invented young man of about seventeen from an island in the New Hebrides. The story follows him in the Mackay district, in Queensland, through to the law of 1901 and the years that came after it.","The story does not name Harry’s island or language, because people from many islands were part of this history and one story cannot speak for all of them."],"arrivalReal":[["landing","real",0],["ship","real",0]],"begin":"how","countryReal":[["creek",0],["creek",2]]}},"behindApp":{"intro":"Everything in the stories is invented, but it is built on real history. This page lists what the time-travel app itself adds. The invented people and events of each era are listed on that era’s own Behind the Story page.","invented":["The time travel. This app does not explain how you travel. It is a device that lets you visit each era, and nothing in the real history says that it can happen.","The visitor (you). You do not have a name or a past in the app. You take the place of the invented person in each story and you return to the present when the story ends.","The hub and the order of the stops. The eras are listed by when each information bank’s history begins. You can visit them in any order.","The wording that opens each visit and the “Your visit is over” message. The author wrote these to join the stories together.","The questions on the Back to Country page. They are the author’s questions, not facts."],"kept":["The four stories are the same stories that were written for the earlier choose-your-path apps. They are kept in the archive folder of each information-bank project on GitHub.","Every scene has a “Real history” panel. Each fact in it has a source. Where the sources disagree, the panel says so.","Images are old photographs, maps or drawings in the public domain, and each one is credited. There are no AI images."]},"about":["Queensland Time Travel joins four choose-your-path stories about Queensland history: the grass rush, the Native Mounted Police, the gold rushes and the sugar industry. Each story was first written as its own app, and each now has a non-fiction information bank. This app links the two.","Use it for interest or in the classroom. Play at least two paths in an era, then read “What really happened” and the information bank. The question to ask each time is: what is invented, and what is real?"],"privacy":"This app has no tracking and no cookies. It uses your browser’s local storage only to remember which eras you have visited on this device. Nothing you do is sent anywhere. The button at the bottom of the hub clears it.","country":{"intro":["You have visited all four eras. In each one, you stood on Country. The land was already the Country of Aboriginal peoples before the people in these stories arrived.","Many of the records you read were written by settlers, officers and officials. They often did not write down what Aboriginal people thought. Aboriginal peoples survived, and their descendants live in Queensland today. This app cannot say what they thought and felt. Only they can say that."],"questions":["Which era changed what you thought? What did the Real history panel tell you that the story did not?","Who is missing from the records you read? How could you find out more, and who should you ask?","Choose one invented person. Which of their choices was the most free, and which was hardly free at all? Give your reasons.","Whose Country do you live on? Find out the name of the people, and ask your teacher how to learn from them."]}},"eras":{"grass":{"id":"grass","repo":"grass-rush-adventure","startChoices":[{"label":"Follow Will, a shepherd on the Darling Downs in 1846","to":"w1"},{"label":"Follow Alex, the manager of a sheep station in central Queensland in 1869","to":"x1"},{"label":"Follow a young Aboriginal person on the Darling Downs in 1840","to":"b1"}],"startText":["In 1840 Europeans began to take up grassland on the Darling Downs, in what is now Queensland, to graze sheep. Over the next sixty years squatters, shepherds, stockmen, selectors and governments changed who held the grass, which animals ate it, and who had a say. The grassland was already the Country of Aboriginal peoples.","In this story you make the choices. You can follow one of three invented people. When a story ends, you can go back and follow one of the others.","The story is invented. The facts are real. Boxes marked “Real history” show the facts and name their sources."],"warning":["This story is about grassland that Aboriginal people had cared for over thousands of years, and about the squatters who took it up for sheep and cattle. It describes violence against Aboriginal people and violence against settlers. It does not describe the violence in detail.","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this app names places where people died, and that the old records it draws on were written by Europeans.","The three main characters are invented. The Aboriginal character has no name and no named family group on purpose. Read “Behind the Story” to see what is real and what is invented.","This app has not yet been reviewed by Aboriginal community members. Review is being sought. Please tell the author if anything needs to change."],"review":null,"sources":{"slqShepherd":{"short":"SLQ, Shepherd Kings of the Darling Downs","t":"State Library of Queensland. “The ‘Shepherd Kings of the Darling Downs’: essay”. From the exhibition Grass Dukes and Shepherd Kings.","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/discover/exhibitions/grass-dukes-shepherd-kings/shepherd-kings-darling-downs-essay","note":"The peoples of the Downs and their burning season, the first squatters, the squatters’ labour force, wool profits, and the decline of the squatters by 1900."},"atf":{"short":"Wallis and Burke, Darling Downs district","t":"Lynley A. Wallis and Heather Burke. “Darling Downs Pastoral District”. Archaeology on the Frontier (website).","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/pastoral-districts/darling-downs/","note":"Dates and numbers of squatters, the count of conflict events, the Native Police camps, and letters and reports from the 1840s and 1850s. The page quotes old letters that contain insulting words about Aboriginal people. I did not copy those words."},"tales":{"short":"Tales from Colonial Queensland, “Reluctant Shepherds”","t":"K. C. Sbeghen. “Reluctant Shepherds”. Tales from Colonial Queensland (blog), 4 September 2013.","u":"http://talesfromcolonialqueensland.blogspot.com/2013/09/reluctant-shepherds.html","note":"Quotes the Moreton Bay Courier of 15 August 1846, 19 September 1846 and 2 January 1847 about hiring shepherds and hut keepers. A blog is a secondary source. I did not read the newspaper pages myself."},"qsaLand":{"short":"QSA, land selection laws","t":"Queensland State Archives. “Part 5: Key Legislation for Land Selections since 1860, summarised”. Stories from the Archives, 3 April 2018.","u":"https://blogs.archives.qld.gov.au/2018/04/03/part-5-key-legislation-for-land-selections-since-1860-summarised/","note":"A summary of the Land Acts of 1860, 1868, 1876, 1884, 1897, 1902, 1906 and 1910, written from research by the historian Ruth Kerr."},"fitzroy":{"short":"Archer, “Fitzroy Waters”","t":"Archibald Archer. “Fitzroy Waters: From Sheep to Cattle and Coal”. Paper read to the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, 24 August 1972. Journal of the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, vol. 9, no. 3.","u":"https://docslib.org/doc/928996/fitzroy-waters-from-sheep-to-cattle-and-coal","note":"Written by a member of the Archer family, who used family letters. I read the first part of the paper on the DocsLib copy. The paper is also held by the University of Queensland library."},"nma":{"short":"National Museum of Australia, Federation Drought","t":"National Museum of Australia. “Federation Drought”. Defining Moments (website), updated 10 June 2025.","u":"https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/federation-drought","note":"Sheep and cattle numbers, stock losses on the droving routes, foreclosures by banks, and the break-up of large stations. Many of its figures are for Australia as a whole. Its Queensland figure is for cattle only."},"ross":{"short":"North Queensland History, Ross River Meatworks","t":"Trisha Fielding. “Ross River Meatworks”. North Queensland History (blog), 26 October 2015.","u":"https://northqueenslandhistory.blogspot.com/2015/10/ross-river-meatworks.html","note":"Dates of building and of the first shipment of frozen beef. Other websites disagree with this blog about the condition of the first shipment when it arrived in London, so this app does not state it."},"slqStrike":{"short":"SLQ blog, Shearers’ Strike 1891","t":"Queensland State Archives (published by the John Oxley Library blog). “Shearers’ Strike 1891”. State Library of Queensland blog, 5 June 2012.","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/shearers-strike-1891","note":"The causes, the centre at Barcaldine, the trials and sentences, and the result of the 1891 strike."},"aph":{"short":"Senate report on stolen wages, chapter 2","t":"Parliament of Australia, Senate Legal and Constitutional Affairs References Committee. “Unfinished business: Indigenous stolen wages”, Chapter 2, “Life under the protection acts”. 2006.","u":"https://www.aph.gov.au/parliamentary_business/committees/senate/legal_and_constitutional_affairs/completed_inquiries/2004-07/stolen_wages/report/c02","note":"The Queensland Act of 1897, the permit and agreement rules for Aboriginal workers, minimum wages in 1901, and estimates by Dr Ros Kidd of the numbers of workers whose labour was controlled."},"newcastle":{"short":"University of Newcastle, Colonial Frontier Massacres, Cullin la Ringo","t":"Centre for 21st Century Humanities, University of Newcastle. “Colonial Frontier Massacres in Central and Eastern Australia 1788–1930”: record for Cullin la Ringo, 17 October 1861.","u":"https://c21ch.newcastle.edu.au/colonialmassacres/detail.php?r=648","note":"A research database. Its record cites newspapers of 1861 and the historians Carment, Reid and Perrin."},"guardian":{"short":"The Guardian, Cullin-la-ringo (2025)","t":"Ella Archibald-Binge. “The massacre of sleeping settlers that unleashed a savage war in central Queensland”. The Guardian Australia, 5 August 2025.","u":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2025/aug/05/cullin-la-ringo-massacre-central-qld-queensland-tom-wills-family-ntwnfb","note":"A news feature that includes the views of Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu people and of descendants of the Wills family. It has a warning about racist language in historical records."},"awKilcoy":{"short":"Australian Wars and Resistance, Kilcoy","t":"Australian Wars and Resistance (website). “Massacre at Kilcoy Station”, 1 February 1842.","u":"https://www.australianwars.net/event.php?id=379","note":"Lists the sources for the Kilcoy poisoning, including the Sydney Morning Herald of 5 December 1842."},"darumbal":{"short":"Darumbal Enterprises","t":"Darumbal Enterprises. “Darumbal History”.","u":"https://darumbal.com.au/darumbal-culture/darumbal-history/","note":"The Darumbal people are the Traditional Owners of the Rockhampton and Capricorn Coast area, including the Fitzroy River catchment."},"abs150":{"short":"ABS, 150 years of Queensland","t":"Australian Bureau of Statistics. “150 Years of Queensland’s Economic History: Key Dates, Facts and Figures”. Qld Stats, February 2009.","u":"https://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs@.nsf/Previousproducts/1318.3Feature%20Article14Feb%202009?issue=Feb+2009&num=&opendocument=&prodno=1318.3&tabname=Summary&view=","note":"Used for the date of separation from New South Wales, 10 December 1859."},"qsaSearch":{"short":"Queensland State Archives, Archives Search","t":"Queensland State Archives. Archives Search (online catalogue).","u":"https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/","note":"For teachers: search here for pastoral lease records, Commissioner of Crown Lands letters and Native Police records. I did not use it for a fact in the story."},"trove":{"short":"Trove","t":"National Library of Australia. Trove: digitised newspapers.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/","note":"For teachers: search for the Moreton Bay Courier (1846 to 1847), the Rockhampton Bulletin (1861) and the Queenslander (1890s)."},"qhr":{"short":"Queensland Heritage Register","t":"Queensland Heritage Register (Department of the Environment, Tourism, Science and Innovation).","u":"https://apps.des.qld.gov.au/heritage-register/","note":"For teachers: search the register for homesteads, woolsheds and meatworks. I did not use any register entry for a fact in the story, because the register pages would not load for my reader."},"qhatlas":{"short":"Queensland Historical Atlas","t":"Queensland Historical Atlas.","u":"https://www.qhatlas.com.au/","note":"For teachers: maps and essays on pastoralism, station homesteads and closer settlement. I did not use it for a fact in the story, because the site would not load for my reader."}},"sourcesIntro":"Every fact in a “Real history” box comes from one of these sources. Newspapers named in the sources can be read on Trove. Queensland State Archives holds the original government records. I read the sources listed here when I wrote this app. Where I did not read a page, the note says so.","imageNote":"All images are old photographs, maps or drawings that are in the public domain. No images in this app were made by artificial intelligence. Some photographs show groups of workers. The sources I read do not say who they were, so the captions do not say either.","images":{"map":{"file":"images/squatting-map-darling-downs-1864.jpg","alt":"An old printed map of the Darling Downs with the boundaries of runs, head stations, townships and roads marked in outline.","caption":"Squatting Map of the Darling Downs District, 1864, by J. W. Buxton. It shows the approximate boundaries of runs and their head stations.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_180739_Squatting_map_of_the_Darling_Downs_district,_1864.jpg","people":false},"gracemere":{"file":"images/gracemere-station-sketch-1869.jpg","alt":"A brown-toned sketch of a lake or river with a small sailing boat, and trees and station buildings on the far bank.","caption":"A sketch of Gracemere Station near Rockhampton, about 1869. Gracemere was a real station. Alex’s station in this story is invented.","credit":"Unknown artist, John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_241830_Sketch_of_Gracemere_Station_near_Rockhampton.jpg","people":false},"yandilla":{"file":"images/sheep-washing-yandilla-1884.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a creek with a large flock of sheep on the bank and men working on a wooden platform and in yards.","caption":"Sheep washing on Yandilla Station, Darling Downs, 1884. Men work in the creek and in the yards.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_295119_Sheep_washing_on_Yandilla_Station,_Darling_Downs,_1884.jpg","people":false},"jimbour":{"file":"images/bullock-team-jimbour-1887.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a team of bullocks in front of large wooden woolsheds.","caption":"A bullock team outside the woolsheds at Jimbour Station, Darling Downs, about 1887.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_43351_Bullock_team_outside_the_woolsheds_at_Jimbour_Station,_Queensland,_ca._1887.jpg","people":false},"shearers":{"file":"images/shearers-1880s.jpg","alt":"A black and white group photograph of many men and boys outside a wooden shearing shed. Some in the front row sit with fleeces.","caption":"Shearers outside a shearing shed, between 1880 and 1890. Four men at the front are shearing with hand shears.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_173975_Portrait_of_shearers_outside_a_shearing_shed,_1880-1890.jpg","people":false}},"nodes":{"w1":{"id":"w1","title":"An agreement to go up country","where":"Brisbane · August 1846","text":["You are Will. You are twenty-two years old. You came to Brisbane by steamer from Sydney with a free fare. In return you signed an agreement to work for a squatter on the Darling Downs.","In town you hear what other men from the ship are doing. Some say the shepherd’s life is lonely and dangerous. Some are trying to find work in the town instead. The squatter’s agent says you will be paid £25 a year as a shepherd, or £22 a year as a hut keeper, with rations.","You must choose which work to do."],"choices":[{"label":"Go up country as a shepherd, for £25 a year","to":"w2","set":{"job":"shepherd"}},{"label":"Go up country as a hut keeper, for £22 a year","to":"w2","set":{"job":"hut"}}],"real":[{"h":"Hiring shepherds, 1846 and 1847","paras":["In 1842 the northern districts of New South Wales, which are now south-east Queensland, were opened to free settlement. Labour for the runs was recruited in Sydney, and recruits were offered a free fare and a bonus.","In 1846 the Moreton Bay Courier reported wages of £25 a year for good shepherds and £22 for hut keepers, with good rations. The paper also complained that some men took the fare and then stayed in Brisbane. In January 1847 it reported that a man who had been hired as a shepherd and refused to go to his station was sentenced to three months’ hard labour. I read these reports as they are quoted in a blog, not on Trove."],"src":["tales"]}]},"w2":{"id":"w2","title":"The outstation","where":"Darling Downs · 1846","img":"map","text":[{"if":{"job":"shepherd"},"t":"You walk out with the flock at dawn. You watch the sheep all day and bring them back to the fold at night. There are no fences. If a sheep strays, you must find it."},{"if":{"job":"hut"},"t":"You look after the hut and the fold. You cook, keep the yards in order, and watch the flock when the shepherd is away. There are no fences."},"The hut is made of slabs and bark. The nearest neighbour is a long day’s ride away. Now and then a dray brings flour, tea and sugar, and the overseer counts the sheep.","In the evenings you hear dogs in the dark. You have heard that Aboriginal people have taken sheep from other runs. You have also heard that some shepherds have been killed.","You have to decide where to graze the flock."],"choices":[{"label":"Keep the flock near the hut","to":"w3","set":{"graze":"near"}},{"label":"Move the flock to better grass, further from the hut","to":"w3","set":{"graze":"far"}}],"real":[{"h":"The Darling Downs, 1840 to 1843","paras":["The Leslie brothers took up runs on the Darling Downs from March 1840. A census recorded 55 European men on the Downs in March 1841, and about 300 people, including a reasonable number of women, in September 1843. The Downs was officially opened for settlement in 1843.","The State Library of Queensland says the first squatters came to sell good raw wool to English mills, and that many were younger sons of English and Scottish gentry. The squatters ruled over a labour force of travelling bush workers, stockmen and shepherds."],"src":["atf","slqShepherd"]},{"h":"Aboriginal people and the grass","paras":["The Downs had been the home, for thousands of years, of the Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples. They practised a burning season to manage the grasses of the plains. Archaeologists Lynley Wallis and Heather Burke write that the grasslands were vital to Aboriginal people and were managed by annual cultural burning."],"src":["slqShepherd","atf"]}]},"w3":{"id":"w3","title":"News from a neighbouring run","where":"Darling Downs · 1848","text":[{"if":{"graze":"near"},"t":"You kept the flock close to the hut. The grass near the hut is eaten short, and some of the sheep are thin."},{"if":{"graze":"far"},"t":"You moved the flock to better grass. The sheep are in good condition, but you are far from the hut."},"One afternoon a rider stops at your hut. He says a shepherd on a run to the west has been killed and the flock driven off. He says the squatters are meeting to send out a party. He does not say what the party will do.","In the next days you hear many stories about what has happened and who did it. You do not know which are true.","You think about whether to stay."],"choices":[{"label":"Stay at the outstation","to":"w4","set":{"risk":"stay"}},{"label":"Ask the overseer to move you to the head station","to":"w4","set":{"risk":"head"}}],"real":[{"h":"Conflict on the Downs","paras":["Wallis and Burke count 92 conflict events in the Darling Downs district. They describe the period from 1840 to the end of the 1850s as almost continuous conflict, and say about 90 per cent of it took place on pastoral runs. The year with the most events was 1848, with 16 events and 27 deaths: 20 Aboriginal people and 7 Europeans.","The Commissioner of Crown Lands, Christopher Rolleston, reported that at least 13 shepherds had been killed between January 1842 and November 1843, and that thousands of sheep had been killed or driven off. The violence also pushed up wages."],"src":["atf"]},{"h":"Violence against Aboriginal people","paras":["Settlers also killed Aboriginal people, in parties led by squatters and in single attacks. On 26 October 1848 three Aboriginal women and a child were killed at Canning Creek. Four people saw it, but all said they could not name those who did it. Rolleston wrote that he could not trace them."],"src":["atf"]}]},"w4":{"id":"w4","title":"Years later","where":"Darling Downs · 1869","text":["Twenty-three years have passed.",{"if":{"risk":"stay"},"t":"You stayed at the outstation for some years, and later worked in the shearing shed."},{"if":{"risk":"head"},"t":"You moved to the head station, and later worked as a stockman and a shearer."},"You have saved some money. Queensland is now a separate colony, and a new law has opened some land to selection. Part of the squatter’s run is being surveyed into blocks. The squatter has already bought the land along the creek.","You could take up a block of your own, or you could stay as a wage worker."],"choices":[{"label":"Take up a selection","to":"e2"},{"label":"Stay as a wage worker","to":"e1"}],"real":[{"h":"Land laws, 1859 to 1884","paras":["Queensland became a separate colony on 10 December 1859. The Crown Lands Alienation Act of 1868 opened land that was not under a pastoral lease to selection. Selectors paid annual rent, and could freehold agricultural land after three years if they met the conditions.","The same Act let pastoralists make pre-emptive selections from their own leases, at one acre for every ten shillings of improvements. Queensland State Archives says this let pastoralists secure land along creek and river banks. The Act of 1876 tried to stop “dummying” by pastoralists, but the Archives says those efforts were unsuccessful. The Lands Act of 1884 abolished pre-emptive purchases."],"src":["abs150","qsaLand"]}]},"e1":{"id":"e1","title":"A wage worker on the run","where":"Darling Downs · after 1869","img":"shearers","text":["You stay. Each year you work at shearing, mustering and mending the yards. The station changes owners. The wage stays about the same. In some years there is work for everyone. In other years there is not.","In 1891 you hear that shearers in central Queensland have gone on strike. You are too old to shear by then, and you hear about it from younger men.","This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible. Many people lived like this."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["At the height of squatter-dominated pastoralism in the 1880s, stations in New South Wales and Queensland were sometimes up to 3,000 square kilometres in size and carried hundreds of thousands of merino sheep.","In 1891 central Queensland was occupied for almost four months with a strike by shearers. Pastoralists had proposed to cut the shearers’ wages, which were then £1 for every hundred sheep shorn, as wool prices fell in the depression of the 1890s. The strike failed, and thirteen union leaders were sentenced to three years on 20 May 1891. Many historians think the strike hastened the call for a political party for working people."],"src":["nma","slqStrike"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"e2":{"id":"e2","title":"A selector’s farm","where":"Darling Downs · after 1869","text":["You choose a block and pay the first year’s rent. You build a hut, clear some land and plant a crop. The block is not on the creek.","In some years the rain comes and the crop grows. In other years it does not. You must pay the rent every year, whether or not the crop grows.","This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["The National Museum of Australia says the eastern colonies passed land laws in the 1860s to control squatters and encourage closer settlement, with the aim of breaking large runs into smaller blocks for farming and grazing. It says this was not always achieved, because squatters found ways to hold on to productive country.","Under the Lands Act of 1884, up to 1,280 acres could be selected as an Agricultural Farm and freeholded after five years, and up to 20,000 acres could be selected as a Grazing Farm and held on a 30-year lease."],"src":["nma","qsaLand"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"x1":{"id":"x1","title":"Improvements or stock","where":"Central Queensland · 1869","img":"gracemere","text":["You are Alex. You are twenty-six years old. You manage a sheep station in central Queensland for an owner who lives in Sydney.","The station has a lease from the Crown for the grass. A new law, the Crown Lands Alienation Act of 1868, has begun to change what the lease means. Parts of the run can be taken back, and the land opened to selectors.","The owner has written to you. He says you may spend money on improvements such as a woolshed and fences. You know that improvements can be turned into land along the river. You also know that sheep cost money, and there is not enough for both."],"choices":[{"label":"Spend the money on improvements, to claim the land along the river","to":"x2","set":{"water":"claimed"}},{"label":"Keep the money for sheep, and hold the lease as it is","to":"x2","set":{"water":"lease"}}],"real":[{"h":"The Land Acts and Gracemere","paras":["Gracemere was a sheep station run by the Archer family near Rockhampton. Archibald Archer, a member of the family, wrote in 1972 that Gracemere was reduced by resumptions after the Land Acts of 1868 and 1869. Rockhampton is in the area that Darumbal Enterprises describes as Darumbal Country.","Under the Act of 1868, pastoralists could make pre-emptive selections from their leases within 12 months of notice of resumption, at one acre for every ten shillings of improvements. Queensland State Archives says this let them secure land along creek and river banks."],"src":["fitzroy","qsaLand","darumbal"]},{"h":"Central Queensland in 1861","paras":["In October 1861, near Springsure, Gayiri people killed 19 members of Horatio Wills’s party at Cullin-la-ringo. A University of Newcastle research database records that two Aboriginal boys had been taken by white men before the attack. It also records one view that the attack was in reprisal for the killing of Gayiri people about a week earlier by Native Police.","Afterwards, settlers and then Native Police killed many Gayiri people. The Guardian reports official records of at least 30 killed on the first day, and a newspaper report that Native Police shot 60 or 70 people. The exact number is not known. Some people at the time claimed it was far larger."],"src":["newcastle","guardian"]}]},"x2":{"id":"x2","title":"Seed in the wool","where":"Central Queensland · 1871","img":"yandilla","text":[{"if":{"water":"claimed"},"t":"You spent the money on a woolshed and fences. The land along the river has been secured for the owner."},{"if":{"water":"lease"},"t":"You kept the money for sheep. Part of the run has been taken back, and the land along the river has been opened to selection."},"The wool is now a problem. At the wool store more bales are marked “seedy”, which means they have grass seed in them. The seed is spreading in the paddocks and the sheep look thin and miserable.","Wool still sells for nearly two shillings a pound, so you do not want to give up the sheep. You need to decide what to do."],"choices":[{"label":"Move the sheep to new country in the west","to":"x2b","set":{"mix":"west"}},{"label":"Put cattle on the paddocks and keep fewer sheep","to":"x3","set":{"mix":"mixed"}},{"label":"Keep the sheep and move them between paddocks","to":"x3","set":{"mix":"sheep"}}],"real":[{"h":"Spear grass at Gracemere","paras":["Archibald Archer quotes an old sheep farmer who said black spear grass overran the country after the wet years of the 1850s, spoiled his wool and made him change his shearing times. Archer says spear grass caused the sheep to leave Gracemere. In the late 1860s the station’s wool book described more and more bales as “seedy”.","In 1871 the manager, James Archer, wrote that the paddocks, after all the expense they had been, were “very little good”. Wool was then nearly two shillings a pound. In 1872 the family bought country at Tambo, and the sheep were driven there between 1872 and 1874. When Robert Stubbs Archer became manager in 1880, there were no sheep left at Gracemere."],"src":["fitzroy"]}]},"x2b":{"id":"x2b","title":"Further west","where":"Central Queensland · 1873","text":["The owner agrees to buy country far to the west. You drive the sheep there over two years. The sheep move slowly and many of the days are dry. At the new run you build yards and a hut.","The new run is also on Country where Aboriginal people already lived. This story does not say what happened to them, because I did not find a record.","Wool from the new run must travel a long way to reach the coast."],"choices":[{"label":"Go on to 1892","to":"x3"}],"real":[{"h":"Moving the sheep, and getting water","paras":["In 1872 the Archer family bought country at Tambo and called it Minnie Downs. The sheep were driven there from Gracemere between 1872 and 1874.","The National Museum of Australia says that later railways and bores, which reached underground water, made it possible for stations to spread further into the dry interior of New South Wales and central Queensland."],"src":["fitzroy","nma"]}]},"x3":{"id":"x3","title":"Two kinds of news","where":"Central Queensland · 1892","img":"jimbour","text":[{"if":{"mix":"west"},"t":"You now manage the station in the west."},{"if":{"mix":"mixed"},"t":"You manage sheep and cattle on the same run."},{"if":{"mix":"sheep"},"t":"You still manage sheep, on the old run."},"Two pieces of news arrive. Last year the shearers went on strike for almost four months, and wool prices are falling. And a freezing works has opened at Townsville, which sends frozen beef to London.","The owner asks what you will do."],"choices":[{"label":"Put more money into cattle for the meat trade","to":"x4","set":{"bet":"cattle"}},{"label":"Keep the sheep and wait for better wool prices","to":"x4","set":{"bet":"sheep"}},{"label":"Borrow from the bank to buy more stock of both kinds","to":"x4","set":{"bet":"borrow"}}],"real":[{"h":"Frozen beef, 1892","paras":["The Ross River Meatworks at Townsville began to be built in August 1891, and by June 1892 the first cattle were being processed. A blog says it was a solution to an oversupply of cattle in the region, and that it employed 700 workers at its peak. The first shipment of frozen beef, 600 tons, left Townsville on the Otarama for London.","Other websites disagree with the blog about the condition of the first shipment when it arrived. This app does not say."],"src":["ross"]},{"h":"The shearers’ strike, 1891","paras":["In 1891 shearers refused to work after pastoralists proposed to cut their wages. Barcaldine became the centre of the strike in February. Thirteen union leaders were sentenced in May to three years in prison. The strike failed."],"src":["slqStrike"]}]},"x4":{"id":"x4","title":"The long dry","where":"Central Queensland · 1898","text":["There has been little rain for three years. The rivers are low. The grass is gone from the paddocks, and the bare ground blows away in the wind.",{"if":{"bet":"cattle"},"t":"You have more cattle than ever."},{"if":{"bet":"sheep"},"t":"You have kept all the sheep."},{"if":{"bet":"borrow"},"t":"You owe the bank a large sum of money, and you have more stock than ever."},"You must decide what to do before another year passes."],"choices":[{"label":"Sell most of the stock now","to":"e3","set":{"sell":"early"}},{"label":"Send the stock along the stock route to find feed","to":"x4b","set":{"sell":"drove"}},{"label":"Hold on and wait for rain","to":"e4","set":{"sell":"hold"}}],"real":[{"h":"The Federation Drought, 1895 to 1903","paras":["The National Museum of Australia says the Federation Drought was the worst in Australia’s history, measured by stock losses. In 1892 Australia had 106 million sheep. By 1903 the flock had almost halved, to 54 million. The country lost more than 40 per cent of its cattle, nearly three million in Queensland alone.","The Museum says the combination of overstocking, drought and rabbits (which it describes for south-eastern Australia) left pastoralists with nothing to feed their stock, and that banks foreclosed on some stations. No state government had a formal drought relief policy. These figures are for the whole country, except the Queensland cattle number."],"src":["nma"]}]},"x4b":{"id":"x4b","title":"On the road","where":"Western Queensland · 1900","text":["You send the stock along the stock route with drovers. There is little feed on the road. The watering places are far apart. Each day some animals die and are left behind.","After some weeks the drovers send word. You must decide whether to sell what is left, or to take the animals back and hold on."],"choices":[{"label":"Sell what is left","to":"e3"},{"label":"Take the animals back and hold on","to":"e4"}],"real":[{"h":"Droving in the drought","paras":["The National Museum of Australia says drovers looked for feed along travelling stock routes, known as the “long paddock”. It says losses of up to 70 per cent were recorded on the road, especially where watering points could be 100 kilometres apart. In 1902 local newspapers reported more than 2,000 steers dead along the Goondiwindi to Miles route in Queensland."],"src":["nma"]}]},"e3":{"id":"e3","title":"A smaller run","where":"Central Queensland · after 1902","text":[{"if":{"sell":"early"},"t":"You sold most of the stock early, at low prices. You kept a few animals and the land along the river."},{"if":{"sell":"drove"},"t":"You sold what was left of the stock after the road. You lost many animals, and you kept a few."},"The bank agrees to let the owner keep part of the run. The owner sells part to the government, which opens it to selectors. You stay on as manager of the smaller run, with fewer sheep and a few cattle. Your new neighbours grow crops and keep small flocks.","This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["Queensland State Archives says the Queensland Agricultural Lands Purchase Act of 1894 let the government buy land from pastoral lessees and open it for selection for family farms. Many of the estates came from owners who were prepared to sell during the depression of the 1890s. The Land Act of 1902 was largely a relief Act for pastoral lessees hit by the drought.","The National Museum of Australia says that by the end of the drought many huge stations had been taken back or foreclosed by banks and divided into smaller holdings. Smaller properties and flocks became normal, and mixed farming was widely taken up."],"src":["qsaLand","nma"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"e4":{"id":"e4","title":"The bank takes the run","where":"Central Queensland · about 1900","text":[{"if":{"sell":"drove"},"t":"You bring back the few animals that survived the road."},"You hold on. The rain does not come for another year. The bank will not lend any more money and takes the run. The lease is taken back and the country is divided into blocks for selectors.","You leave with your clothes, your horse and a letter from the owner. Many other station managers and owners are in the same position.","This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["The National Museum of Australia says banks foreclosed on some stations and many graziers walked off their land. The Museum says the drought ended squatter-dominated pastoralism in New South Wales and Queensland, as bank foreclosures and the resumption of leases led to the partition of large stations.","Queensland State Archives says that much pastoral land leased under the Pastoral Leases Act of 1869 was deserted in the drought. The State Library of Queensland says the squatters’ way of life on the Darling Downs lasted less than fifty years."],"src":["nma","qsaLand","slqShepherd"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"b1":{"id":"b1","title":"The burning season","where":"Darling Downs · 1840","text":["You are a young Aboriginal person living on the Darling Downs. This story gives you no name and no family group, on purpose. See “Behind the Story” to learn why.","It is the time of year when the grass is burned. The grass is managed in this way on the plains. People have cared for this grassland for thousands of years.","This year something is different. Strangers have come up from the south on horses, with flocks of sheep and men to watch them. They have built a hut. There are not many yet, but more are coming.","You see the strangers’ flock near a waterhole."],"choices":[{"label":"Watch from a distance","to":"b2","set":{"contact":"watch"}},{"label":"Go near the strangers’ camp","to":"b2","set":{"contact":"near"}}],"real":[{"h":"The Downs in 1840","paras":["The State Library of Queensland says the Keinjan, Giabal, Jarowair and Barunggam peoples had lived on the Downs for thousands of years, and practised a burning season to manage the grasses of the plains. The Leslie brothers took up runs from March 1840. A census recorded only 55 European men on the Downs in March 1841."],"src":["slqShepherd","atf"]}]},"b2":{"id":"b2","title":"Sheep at the waterhole","where":"Darling Downs · 1841 to 1843","text":[{"if":{"contact":"watch"},"t":"You watch from a distance. You see how many men there are, and what they do."},{"if":{"contact":"near"},"t":"You go near the strangers’ camp. The men do not welcome you."},"In the next months more strangers arrive. Sheep eat the grass and drink at the waterholes. Some of your people take sheep from the flocks. Others say this will bring trouble.","Men on horses ride to some camps with guns. You hear that at a place called Kilcoy, a long way to the east, many people ate flour that had poison in it, and that many died.","People in the camps talk about what to do. They do not all agree."],"choices":[{"label":"Move away, to the mountains in the north","to":"b3","set":{"path":"mountains"}},{"label":"Stay on the plains and keep out of the way of the stations","to":"b3","set":{"path":"stay"}},{"label":"Join those who fight to keep the sheep off the waterholes","to":"b3","set":{"path":"fight"}}],"real":[{"h":"Reprisal and resistance, 1841 to 1844","paras":["In November 1841 George Leslie wrote to his parents that Aboriginal people had killed or carried away about 60 young ewes, and that the Leslies had “given them what they will not forget in a hurry”. Wallis and Burke read this as a major reprisal. In 1843 and 1844 they describe a state of open warfare across the Downs, in which Aboriginal people deliberately targeted rations and supplies.","Wallis and Burke also note that the violence was costly for squatters, in lives, stock and wages."],"src":["atf"]},{"h":"Kilcoy, 1842","paras":["On 1 February 1842, at Kilcoy station, flour mixed with strychnine was given to a large group of Aboriginal people by two shepherds employed by the lessee, Evan MacKenzie. At least sixty people died. The record cites the Sydney Morning Herald of 5 December 1842. Kilcoy is east of the Darling Downs, not on the Downs. According to a history by Lauer that the page cites, two escaped convicts reported that 14 or 15 Aboriginal groups met in the Bunya scrub to discuss a response."],"src":["awKilcoy"]}]},"b3":{"id":"b3","title":"The Native Police","where":"Darling Downs · 1849 to 1853","text":[{"if":{"path":"mountains"},"t":"You went to the mountains for a time, and now you have come back to the edge of the plains."},{"if":{"path":"stay"},"t":"You stayed on the plains and kept out of the way of the stations."},{"if":{"path":"fight"},"t":"You fought to keep the sheep off the waterholes. Many of your people have been killed."},"In 1849 a new force arrives. The troopers are Aboriginal men from far to the south, led by a white commandant. They are called the Native Police. They carry guns and ride horses. They have a camp near the border.","Later some squatters pay for barracks for the troopers. In a few years the newspapers write that the first fierce fighting is over. The squatters say the district is quiet."],"choices":[{"label":"Keep away from the troopers and the stations","to":"b4","set":{"end":"away"}},{"label":"Take work at a station for rations","to":"b4","set":{"end":"work"}}],"real":[{"h":"The Native Police on the Downs","paras":["Commandant Frederick Walker set up the first Native Police camp in what became Queensland in May 1849, at Callandoon, on the present border. He had 14 troopers recruited from the Murrumbidgee and Murray River areas of southern New South Wales and northern Victoria. In 1851 squatters built barracks at Wandai Gumbal, near the present Condamine, at their own expense, after asking for help.","In 1858 a squatter told a New South Wales committee that his district became quiet within three months of the Native Police arriving, and that his land became fifty per cent more valuable. Wallis and Burke cite a newspaper of November 1853 that said the first fierce resistance had been “subdued”, and they say violence was still occurring sporadically."],"src":["atf"]},{"h":"The Commissioner’s view","paras":["Christopher Rolleston, the Commissioner of Crown Lands from 1843, tried to befriend local Aboriginal people by giving blankets, tomahawks and clothes, and by encouraging settlers to employ them. Wallis and Burke, citing the historian M. Shield, say this was not often successful because Aboriginal people did not trust Europeans after violence by shepherds and stockmen against Aboriginal women."],"src":["atf"]}]},"b4":{"id":"b4","title":"After the fighting","where":"Darling Downs · the 1850s and after","text":[{"if":{"end":"away"},"t":"You keep away from the stations and the troopers. There is less land where you can go without being seen."},{"if":{"end":"work"},"t":"You take work at a station, in return for rations. You are not paid wages. The people who give the orders are the squatters."},"The grassland that your people burned each year now feeds sheep and cattle. The runs now have head stations and are shown on maps.","The frontier moves north and west. The same things happen to other peoples, in other places."],"choices":[{"label":"See what the records say about later years","to":"e5"}],"real":[{"h":"The Downs after the 1850s","paras":["Wallis and Burke write that by the 1850s the squatters had pushed outwards and that Aboriginal defiance had worn down in the southern Downs. By 1877 the Jondaryan run on the Downs was the largest freehold run in Queensland, owned by Edward Wienholt and the trustees of William Kent."],"src":["atf"]},{"h":"The frontier moves north, 1861","paras":["In October 1861 a cycle of killings and reprisals took place near Springsure in central Queensland. See the box “Central Queensland in 1861” in Alex’s story. The Guardian reports that Aboriginal people who remained in that region were taken to missions or reserves from the early 1900s, under laws said to protect them."],"src":["newcastle","guardian"]}]},"e5":{"id":"e5","title":"What the records leave out","where":"Queensland · 1897 and after","text":[{"if":{"end":"away"},"t":"You and your family keep away from the stations for as long as you can."},{"if":{"end":"work"},"t":"You and your family work on stations in return for rations."},"This story is invented. Aboriginal people of the Darling Downs are real. They survived, and their descendants live in Queensland today. This story cannot say what they thought and felt. Only they can say that."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["In 1897 Queensland passed the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act. Under it, an Aboriginal worker could be employed only under a permit from a protector, and employers had to sign an agreement for no more than 12 months. In 1901 a minimum wage of five shillings was set for most workers under the Act, which the Senate committee says was less than one-eighth of the “white wage”. From 1904 the wages of Aboriginal workers could be paid to protectors.","Dr Ros Kidd told the Senate committee that between 4,000 and 5,500 pastoral workers in Queensland each year, from the 1920s to the 1960s, had their labour controlled by the government. She said this was a “guesstimate” and was probably much too low."],"src":["aph"]}],"end":true,"kind":""}},"behind":{"sections":[{"kind":"made","h":"What is invented","p":["All of the story text is invented. The facts are in the boxes marked “Real history”."],"list":["Will, his age, his hut, his flock, his choices and his savings are invented. The wages he is offered, and the case of a shepherd who was sentenced for refusing to go to his station, are real, but Will is not any real shepherd.","Alex, the owner in Sydney, Alex’s station and every decision Alex makes are invented. Gracemere is a real station, but Alex does not manage it. Facts about Gracemere are used as an example of what happened on central Queensland stations.","The young Aboriginal person on the Downs is invented. I have deliberately given no name and no family group, because I do not know who the person’s people were and I do not want to invent a name or customs.","The news that a shepherd has been killed and a party sent out in 1848 is invented. It stands for events that archaeologists have recorded on the Downs. It is not a record of a real event.","The scene at the waterhole, the choices of the unnamed person, and the arrival of the Native Police in the person’s life are invented. The Native Police camps are real.","The statement in the ending that the person worked for rations, or kept away from the stations, is an assumption by the author for the 1850s, not a record.","Every ending is invented. They show lives that were possible, not lives that were recorded.","No real person speaks in the story. Real people are named only in the fact boxes."]},{"kind":"real","h":"What is real","list":["The Leslie brothers took up runs on the Darling Downs from March 1840, and the Downs was officially opened for settlement in 1843.","The wages for shepherds and hut keepers in 1846, and the sentence passed in January 1847 on a hired shepherd who refused to go to his station.","Conflict on the Downs from 1840 to the end of the 1850s, the count of 92 events, the 13 shepherds recorded as killed from 1842 to 1843, the killing at Canning Creek on 26 October 1848, the poisoning at Kilcoy on 1 February 1842, and the Native Police camps from 1849.","Queensland’s separation on 10 December 1859 and the land laws of 1868, 1876, 1884, 1894 and 1902.","Gracemere’s change from sheep to cattle between the 1860s and 1880, including the spear grass, the move of the sheep to Tambo from 1872 to 1874, and James Archer’s words of 1871.","The Ross River Meatworks (1891 to 1892), the shearers’ strike of 1891, and the Federation Drought of 1895 to 1903.","The events at Cullin-la-ringo in October 1861 and the killings that followed, as recorded in the sources named.","Queensland’s Aboriginal employment laws from 1897."]},{"kind":"real","h":"What I could not find, or what historians do not agree on","list":["I did not read the 1846 and 1847 newspaper reports myself. I read them as quoted by a blog.","I did not find a source for what shepherds were paid in rations. The story uses the general word “rations” and nothing more exact.","I did not find a source for the Aboriginal people who lived near the new station in the west, or at Gracemere, apart from the Darumbal Enterprises page about Rockhampton.","Sources disagree about the condition of the first shipment of frozen beef from Townsville in 1892, so the story does not say.","The numbers of Aboriginal people killed at Cullin-la-ringo and afterwards are not known. The sources give figures from 30 to about 300. I have given the figures with their sources.","Kilcoy is east of the Darling Downs, not on the Downs. I included it because of its connection to the region.","I did not read the Queensland Historical Atlas or the Queensland Heritage Register for any fact, because they would not load.","I read the National Museum of Australia’s figures for sheep and cattle as figures for Australia as a whole. Only the figure “nearly three million cattle” is for Queensland alone.","The word “dummying” is explained from general knowledge. The Archives page I read does not define it. Please check the meaning."]},{"kind":"care","h":"Care with Aboriginal history, and cultural review","p":["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this app mentions places where people died. It describes violence against Aboriginal people and violence against settlers.","The old records often use insulting words about Aboriginal people. Some of the web pages named in the sources quote them. I have not copied those words into this app. I used the words “Aboriginal people” and “Native Police”. The Native Police were Aboriginal men, usually from other parts of Australia, who served under European officers and were used to break up Aboriginal camps.","The Aboriginal path has been written without names or customs of any people. The author wants to ask Traditional Owners and Aboriginal community members to review this app before it is used widely. The people to ask include the Giabal, Jarowair, Keinjan and Barunggam peoples for the Darling Downs, the Gayiri, Bidjara and Ghungalu peoples for the Springsure area, and the Darumbal people for Rockhampton. Until that review is done, please treat this app as a draft."]},{"kind":"real","h":"Words in this story","p":["A “squatter” was a person who grazed stock on Crown land, at first without a lease. A “run” was the area of land a squatter held. A “station” was the run together with its buildings and workers. A “head station” was the main set of buildings on a run. An “outstation” was a hut and yards a long way from the head station. A “shepherd” watched a flock of sheep. A “hut keeper” looked after a hut and its yards. A “stockman” worked with cattle and horses. A “selector” was a person who took up a block of land under a Land Act. A “lease” was the right to use Crown land for a set time. “Pre-emptive purchase” was the right of a pastoralist to buy land from his own lease. “Resumption” was the government taking back part of a lease. “Dummying” was selecting land on behalf of someone else, against the law. A “merino” is a breed of sheep with fine wool. “Dispersal” was the word used for breaking up an Aboriginal camp by force."]}]},"discuss":[{"h":"Understand","qs":[{"q":"What was a squatter? Why did squatters take up runs on the Darling Downs in the 1840s?"},{"q":"Describe the work of a shepherd or hut keeper in 1846. What were the dangers? What were the pay and the rules?"},{"q":"What did the Crown Lands Alienation Act of 1868 do? Who gained and who lost?"}]},{"h":"Think and discuss","qs":[{"q":"Archibald Archer says spear grass caused the sheep to leave Gracemere. What other reasons for changing from sheep to cattle might there have been? What evidence would you need?"},{"q":"Wallis and Burke use letters and newspapers written by squatters. Whose voices are missing? How does that change what we can know?"},{"q":"The National Museum says the Federation Drought ended squatter-dominated pastoralism. What caused the large losses of stock? Which of its figures are for Queensland?"},{"q":"Wallis and Burke call 1840 to the end of the 1850s a period of almost continuous conflict on the Downs. At Canning Creek no-one could be found who would name those who killed three Aboriginal women and a child. What does this show about the law at the time?"},{"q":"Compare Will and Alex. What choices did each have? What choices did each not have? Who made the decisions that affected them?"}]},{"h":"Write","qs":[{"q":"Write a diary entry for Will, for Alex or for the unnamed person. Use only facts from the Real history boxes. Mark any sentence that is invented."},{"q":"Write a paragraph for or against this statement: “The land laws of the 1860s broke up the power of the squatters.” Use two facts."},{"q":"What would you want to ask Traditional Owners before telling this story to a class? List three questions."}]}]},"np":{"id":"np","repo":"native-police-adventure","startChoices":null,"warning":["This resource may contain names and images of deceased Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander people.","It is about the Native Police and frontier violence in Queensland. It does not show violence in detail, but it does describe it. You can stop at any time. If the story upsets you, talk to a teacher, a family member or someone you trust."],"review":"Review status: this resource has not yet been reviewed by Traditional Owners. Teachers, please seek advice from the Traditional Owners of your area before using it in class. In Central Queensland they include, for example, the Darumbal, Gaangalu and Wulli Wulli peoples.","sources":{"burke":{"t":"Burke, H., Barker, B., Cole, N., Wallis, L.A., Hatte, E., Davidson, I. and Lowe, K. (2018). The Queensland Native Police and Strategies of Recruitment on the Queensland Frontier, 1849–1901. Journal of Australian Studies.","u":"https://doi.org/10.1080/14443058.2018.1474942","short":"Burke and others 2018"},"nulungu":{"t":"Nulungu Research Institute, University of Notre Dame Australia. Rewriting the history of the Native Mounted Police in Queensland (Nulungu Insights).","u":"https://researchonline.nd.edu.au/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1000&context=nulungu_insights","short":"Nulungu Insights"},"fcdb":{"t":"Frontier Conflict and the Native Mounted Police in Queensland. Research database of camps, officers, troopers, conflict events and primary sources, 1848–1904.","u":"https://doi.org/10.25957/5d9fb541294d5","short":"Frontier Conflict database"},"qsa":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. Agency A2377, Native Police.","u":"https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/agencies/A2377","short":"Queensland State Archives, agency A2377"},"georgey":{"t":"Queensland State Archives, QSA846769. The Petition of Georgey, 6 May 1863 (transcribed by the Frontier Conflict project). Printed in the Courier (Brisbane), 7 May 1863, page 3, available through Trove.","u":"https://fromthepage.com/qldfrontierconflict/correspondence-1879/qsa846769-1863-the-petition-of-georgy-6-may-colonial-secretarys-office-in-letters-63-934","short":"Petition of Georgey, 1863"},"sc1861":{"t":"Queensland Legislative Assembly (1861). Report from the Select Committee on the Native Police Force and the Condition of the Aborigines Generally. Brisbane: Fairfax and Belbridge. Held by the National Library of Australia.","u":"https://nla.gov.au/nla.obj-52862431/view","short":"Select Committee report, 1861"},"massacres":{"t":"University of Newcastle. Colonial Frontier Massacres in Central and Eastern Australia 1788–1930 (map and Introduction).","u":"https://c21ch.newcastle.edu.au/colonialmassacres/introduction.php","short":"Colonial Frontier Massacres map, Newcastle"},"ryan":{"t":"Ryan, L. (2019–20). Colonial frontier massacres. Notes for a Parliamentary Library seminar, Parliament of Australia.","u":"https://www.aph.gov.au/-/media/05_About_Parliament/54_Parliamentary_Depts/544_Parliamentary_Library/Seminars/2019-20/LyndallRyanNotes.pdf","short":"Ryan, Parliamentary Library notes"},"woor":{"t":"Queensland Government. Community histories: Woorabinda.","u":"https://www.qld.gov.au/firstnations/cultural-awareness-heritage-arts/community-histories/community-histories-u-y/community-histories-woorabinda","short":"Queensland Government, Woorabinda history"},"darumbal":{"t":"Darumbal Enterprises. Darumbal History.","u":"https://darumbal.com.au/darumbal-culture/darumbal-history/","short":"Darumbal Enterprises"},"aww":{"t":"Australian Wars and Resistance. Rockhampton region entry.","u":"https://www.australianwars.net/details.php?id=2503","short":"Australian Wars and Resistance"},"blog":{"t":"Wallis, L., Burke, H., Barker, B. and Cole, N. (2018). Willing Volunteers? Recruiting Aboriginal Boys and Men to the NMP, Part II. Archaeology on the Frontier. This page quotes the Rockhampton Bulletin, 1 February 1873, page 2 (Trove).","u":"https://archaeologyonthefrontier.com/2018/05/13/willing-volunteers/","short":"Archaeology on the Frontier blog"},"photo":{"t":"Photograph of Lieutenant George Murray and his detachment of Native Mounted Police, Rockhampton, 1864. Queensland Police Museum, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain in Australia).","u":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queensland_Native_Police_1864.jpg","short":"Photograph, Rockhampton 1864"}},"sourcesIntro":null,"imageNote":"The only image in the app is one photograph from 1864. It is in the public domain in Australia and shows men who have died. You can choose whether to view it. There are no AI images and no drawings made for this app.","images":{"troopers":{"file":"images/native-mounted-police-rockhampton-1864.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of an officer, a camp sergeant and troopers of the Native Mounted Police standing in two rows at Rockhampton in 1864.","caption":"Lieutenant George Murray and his detachment of Native Mounted Police at Rockhampton, 1864. The back row shows Trooper Carbine, Lieutenant Murray, a second lieutenant and a camp sergeant whose names are not recorded, and Corporal Michael. The front row shows Troopers Barney, Hector, Goondallie, Balantyne and Patrick. These are the names used for the men in the force. Photograph held by the Queensland Police Museum, public domain.","credit":"","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Queensland_Native_Police_1864.jpg","people":true,"summary":"the 1864 photograph of Native Mounted Police at Rockhampton (it shows men who have died)"},"quay":{"file":"images/rockhampton-quay-1864.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a river bank with ships tied up at a wharf. A few low buildings stand beside a wide dirt street on the right, and a verandah post stands in the front left.","caption":"Ships docked at the wharf in Quay Street, Rockhampton, 1864.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_51152_Ships_docked_at_the_wharf_in_Quay_Street,_Rockhampton,_1864.jpg","people":false}},"nodes":{"how":{"id":"how","title":"Before you ride","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["This story is set in Central Queensland in the late 1870s. You are about seventeen years old. The white officers call you Billy. That is a name the force gave you. You have another name from your own country, and you do not say it here.","Your own country is a long way to the south. You are a trooper in the Native Mounted Police. This is a police force of Aboriginal men who are led by white officers.","How did you come to be a trooper?"],"choices":[{"label":"You were taken from a station when you were about fourteen.","to":"camp","set":{"how":"taken"}},{"label":"You were in jail, and you were told the force was the other choice.","to":"camp","set":{"how":"sentence"}},{"label":"You said yes, because you saw no other way to stay alive.","to":"camp","set":{"how":"joined"}}],"real":[{"paras":["The records name several ways that men came into the force. These include force, kidnapping, offers such as a shorter prison sentence, and joining that may have looked like a free choice. In 1878 the Executive Council approved cutting prison sentences for Aboriginal men who joined."],"src":["burke"]},{"paras":["A parliamentary committee in 1861 heard that boys were being taken by force from inland stations."],"src":["burke","sc1861"]},{"paras":["No account is known from any trooper of how he was recruited. The people who wrote the records were the officers and settlers."],"src":["burke"]},{"paras":["In the early years troopers were often brought from far away, so that they had no family ties to the people they were sent against. By the 1870s troopers were more often posted nearer their home region. A trooper could still be a stranger in another nation’s country, even a short distance from home."],"src":["burke"]},{"paras":["A Rockhampton newspaper reported in February 1873 that eleven men were enlisted in one week, chosen from a large number who had “presented themselves”. The researchers who quote this say it is unlikely that many recruits were truly willing."],"src":["blog"]}]},"camp":{"id":"camp","title":"Morning at the camp","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":[{"if":{"how":"taken"},"t":"You remember the day the troopers rode up to the station. Nobody asked you whether you wanted to come."},{"if":{"how":"sentence"},"t":"You remember the magistrate’s room. The choice was jail or the force, and you chose the force."},{"if":{"how":"joined"},"t":"You remember going to the officer yourself. You wanted a horse, food and a place to sleep. Nobody can say that you were forced, and nobody can say that you were free."},"The camp is beside a creek. There are slab huts for the officer and the sergeant, a yard for the horses and a long shed where the troopers sleep. Eight troopers live here. Sub-Inspector Hale is in charge. Camp Sergeant Doyle looks after the stores and gives the orders when Hale is away.","This morning the camp is busy before sunrise. A rider came in overnight from Ridge Creek station with a letter. Hale has been in his hut with it for an hour."],"choices":[{"label":"Go to the horse yard and check your gear.","to":"horses"},{"label":"Find Corporal Tom at the creek.","to":"corporal"}],"real":[{"paras":["A Native Police camp usually had four to twelve troopers under one European officer. One or two white men might also live at the camp as camp keepers."],"src":["nulungu"]},{"paras":["When Queensland separated from New South Wales in 1859, the force had a commandant, lieutenants, camp sergeants and about 100 Aboriginal troopers. Its headquarters were at Rockhampton. A barracks was built at Rockhampton in 1860 and another on the Nogoa River in 1861."],"src":["qsa","woor"]},{"paras":["Troopers were given English names while in the force, and many of these names were insulting. Only some troopers kept their own names in the records."],"src":["burke"]}]},"horses":{"id":"horses","title":"The horse yard","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["You saddle your horse and clean your carbine. A carbine is a short rifle that is carried on the saddle. You look after horses well, and you are the best tracker in the detachment. Hale knows this, and so does the sergeant.","Sergeant Doyle walks along the rail. “Ammunition from the store hut, all of you,” he says. “Hale wants us on the road within the hour.”","The other troopers do not talk much. They check their saddles and watch the officer’s hut."],"choices":[{"label":"Collect your ammunition and wait for the officer.","to":"briefing"}],"real":[{"paras":["Troopers wore uniforms, rode horses and carried guns. Settlers and newspapers wrote that these things attracted recruits. Researchers say the uniform and equipment also showed who held power. A newspaper in 1853 reported that recruits had left because they were kept at drill for six months without uniforms, saddles or arms."],"src":["burke"]},{"paras":["Skill at tracking was valued. A newspaper writer in 1879 said troopers were chosen for “pluck and tracking prowess”."],"src":["burke"]}]},"corporal":{"id":"corporal","title":"By the creek","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["Corporal Tom is washing his shirt in the creek. He has served in the force for six years. That is longer than anyone else in the camp.","“There is a letter from a station,” he says. “Hale will use the word dispersal. Listen for it.”","You know what the word means. Everyone in the camp knows. In an officer’s report it sounds like a patrol sending people away. In the camp it means people are shot.","“Two men left in the night last season,” Tom says quietly. “They were not found. I did not see them go.” He looks at you. “I am telling you that I did not see.”","He picks up his shirt and walks back to the yard."],"choices":[{"label":"Go to the store hut and wait for the officer.","to":"briefing"}],"real":[{"paras":["Official papers used the word “disperse” for the shooting and killing of groups of Aboriginal people. It was a polite word that hid what happened."],"src":["nulungu","burke"]},{"paras":["Of 181 troopers named by the historian Lesley Skinner, 59 deserted or were discharged. That is 32 per cent. Sometimes a whole detachment left together."],"src":["burke"]}]},"briefing":{"id":"briefing","title":"The briefing","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["Hale comes out at last and stands on the verandah with the letter. He reads it aloud while Doyle watches the troopers.","The owner of Ridge Creek station says that one of his stockmen was found dead beside a waterhole two days ago. About forty cattle are missing. Nobody saw what happened. The owner asks for the police to find the people responsible and to disperse them.","“We ride at once,” Hale says. “You will do as you are told. If I am not there, the sergeant gives the orders. Billy, you will track.”","Hale folds the letter. Nobody speaks. You have a question, and you do not know whether to ask it."],"choices":[{"label":"Say nothing. Mount up.","to":"ride"},{"label":"Ask Hale what the order means for women, children and people who have done nothing.","to":"ask"}],"real":[{"paras":["The main work of the Native Police was to answer settlers’ requests to “disperse” Aboriginal people in newly settled districts. The force moved north and west as new areas were taken up."],"src":["nulungu"]},{"paras":["Native Police often travelled with settlers who were taking up new pastoral runs in Central Queensland. Pastoral settlement there grew from the late 1840s, after Ludwig Leichhardt’s expedition passed through in 1844."],"src":["woor"]}]},"ask":{"id":"ask","title":"Your question","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","set":{"asked":true},"text":["You step forward. It is not usual for a trooper to speak to the officer in front of the others.","“Sir, when we find the camp, who do we take? Only the men who did it?”","Hale looks at you for some time. “You will find them first,” he says. “Then I will decide. Do not ask me in front of the sergeant again.”","Doyle’s mouth is tight. Tom looks at the ground. Hale has not answered your question. You mount your horse."],"choices":[{"label":"Ride to Ridge Creek station.","to":"ride"}],"real":[{"paras":["In 1861 a parliamentary committee in Queensland looked into charges of “unnecessary cruelty” by Native Police officers. It still recommended keeping the force, with better officers, discipline and reporting."],"src":["sc1861"]}]},"ride":{"id":"ride","title":"Ridge Creek station","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["The ride takes two days. On the afternoon of the second day the detachment reaches the station. It has a slab homestead, stockyards and a long paddock that runs down to a waterhole.","The owner, Mr Carver, comes to the gate with his overseer. “That waterhole is the only good water on my run,” he says. “The cattle drink there. The local people have camped near it for as long as anyone knows, and they will not move off.”","He shows you where the stockman was found. You look at the ground. You see the marks of cattle and shod horses. You see that the cattle have trampled the edge of the water and the old camping places.","“Find them,” says Carver."],"choices":[{"label":"Start tracking.","to":"tracks"}],"real":[{"paras":["Historian Lyndall Ryan notes that frontier massacres usually took place in reprisal for the killing of a colonist, or for the alleged theft of livestock that had moved onto Aboriginal hunting grounds."],"src":["ryan"]},{"paras":["The Darumbal people, whose country includes the Rockhampton district, say that from the 1850s many of their people were driven from their land, and that survivors were later moved to missions such as Woorabinda, about 200 kilometres away."],"src":["darumbal"]}]},"tracks":{"id":"tracks","title":"The path to the spring","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["You follow the marks on the ground for a day and a half. You can tell many things from them: how many people walked there, how fast, and whether anyone carried small children. The path goes up a dry creek to a place with a spring and a stand of trees.","At dusk, from a ridge, you see smoke. You count the fires. There are about twenty people. There are older men, women and children, and some young men. A few spears lean against a tree. You see no cattle. You see nothing that belonged to the stockman.","Sergeant Doyle rides up beside you. “Well?” he says. “Is it them?”","You do not know whether these are the people who camped at the waterhole. You do not know whether any of them had anything to do with the stockman. Doyle is waiting for your answer."],"choices":[{"label":"Tell Doyle exactly what you see.","to":"report"},{"label":"Say the marks have gone cold, and lead the patrol away.","to":"wrong"},{"label":"Say you need a closer look, and go on alone after dark.","to":"warn"}],"real":[{"paras":["Aboriginal people resisted the loss of their country. In 1855 large groups of people attacked a Native Police camp and then a station in the Rockhampton region. One modern source describes these events as an alliance of the Gangalu, Darumbal, Bayali and Gureng Gureng peoples."],"src":["aww"]},{"paras":["Frontier massacres in Queensland were most common in the 1860s and 1870s. In the Colonial Frontier Massacres project a massacre means the deliberate killing of six or more undefended people in one operation."],"src":["massacres"]}]},"report":{"id":"report","title":"Your report","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["You tell Doyle what you saw: the fires, the number of people and where the spring is. Doyle rides back to Hale.","Hale does not ask whether it is the right camp. “We go in before first light,” he says. “Troopers on the left and the right. Sergeant, you take the middle. Nobody fires until I give the word.”","You understand that there will be a word."],"choices":[{"label":"Lie down and wait for the morning.","to":"night"}],"real":[{"paras":["Frontier massacres were usually planned, not sudden. They happened in secret, and no witnesses were meant to be there."],"src":["ryan"]}]},"night":{"id":"night","title":"Before dawn","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["You lie on your blanket and do not sleep. At about three in the morning Corporal Tom sits down next to you.","“I have done this before,” he says. “I will not tell you what to do. I will tell you that once it is done, it stays done. You will remember it for the rest of your life.”","The horses are saddled. In an hour it will be light."],"choices":[{"label":"Ride in with the others when Hale gives the order.","to":"end_report"},{"label":"Tell Hale you will not go in.","to":"arrest","set":{"why":"refused"}}],"real":[{"paras":["Researchers describe the lasting harm to trooper families and to Aboriginal communities. They use the idea of historical trauma to explain how violence in one generation affects the next."],"src":["burke"]}]},"wrong":{"id":"wrong","title":"The wrong path","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","set":{"via":"wrong"},"text":["You tell Doyle the marks are going cold. You say the ground is hard and that the last rain has washed them out. You say it plainly and you do not look at him.","You lead the patrol east along a stony ridge. It is the wrong way. For two days you ride in a wide circle.","On the third day Hale halts the patrol and looks at the dry creek bed, and then at you. “The marks are gone,” you say. “Perhaps,” he says. He turns the patrol back to the station that afternoon."],"choices":[{"label":"Ride back to the station with the patrol.","to":"empty"}],"real":[{"paras":["In 1861 Frederick Carr, a Native Police officer, told a parliamentary committee that harsh treatment was the main reason troopers deserted. He said: “Many desert from disliking their officers.”"],"src":["burke","sc1861"]}]},"warn":{"id":"warn","title":"Alone in the dark","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["You tell Doyle you need to see how many people there are. He grunts and lets you go.","You leave your horse tied below the ridge and walk down in the dark. You carry nothing in your hands. You know that if the patrol finds out, you will be punished."],"choices":[{"label":"Walk down to the spring.","to":"council"}],"real":[{"paras":["No record is known of a trooper warning a camp. This scene is invented. The story includes it because the records show only the officers’ side, and a story can ask what else might have happened."],"src":["burke"]}]},"council":{"id":"council","title":"At the spring","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["Two young men see you first and pick up spears. You show them your empty hands. An old woman comes out of the shadows. She looks at your jacket, your boots and the buttons. She does not look afraid.","A young man who has worked on stations speaks some English, and he repeats her words to you. “She says she knows who you are. She says the police have come before. The people have learned what the sound of the horses means. They already planned to leave at first light.”","The old woman speaks again, and the young man waits before he translates. “She asks which side you are on.”","You do not have an easy answer. You tell them the patrol is on the ridge and that it will come in at dawn. The old woman nods once. She has decided what to do, and she did not need you to tell her."],"choices":[{"label":"Ask if you can go with them.","to":"end_stand"},{"label":"Go back before the patrol knows you have gone.","to":"empty","set":{"via":"warned"}}],"real":[{"paras":["Survivors of Native Police operations in Central Queensland were later often used as cheap labour on pastoral stations, or lived on the edges of settlements."],"src":["woor"]},{"paras":["Troopers who deserted feared the people of the country they had to cross. A trooper named Georgey said in a petition in 1863 that he was afraid to be left in the bush, far from his own people, among people who hated his people."],"src":["georgey"]}]},"empty":{"id":"empty","title":"An empty place","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":[{"if":{"via":"warned"},"t":"You get back to your horse before the sky turns grey. At first light the patrol rides in. The camp at the spring is empty. The fires are still warm. There are footprints going up into the stony ranges where horses cannot follow."},{"if":{"via":"wrong"},"t":"At the station Mr Carver is not pleased. He tells Hale that his cattle will still be taken and that the force is no use to him."},"Hale writes his report that night. Doyle stands near you and speaks to Hale in a voice that you can hear. “That tracker,” he says. “I am not sure about him.”",{"if":{"asked":true},"t":"He remembers that you asked a question in front of the whole detachment."},"Hale does not answer. He looks at you for a long time."],"choices":[{"label":"Say nothing.","to":"end_empty"},{"label":"Tell Hale what you did, because you cannot keep lying.","to":"arrest","set":{"why":"confessed"}}],"real":[{"paras":["Native Police detachments moved north and west as the frontier moved, and the work of “dispersal” began again in each new district."],"src":["nulungu"]}]},"arrest":{"id":"arrest","title":"In irons","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":[{"if":{"why":"refused"},"t":"“I will not ride in,” you say. You say it where the other troopers can hear. Hale answers in a flat voice. “You are refusing an order.”"},{"if":{"why":"confessed"},"t":"You tell Hale what you did. Hale listens until you finish. “You lied to me,” he says. “You lied in front of my men.”"},"Doyle takes your carbine and puts irons on your wrists. “Disobedience,” he says. “It will be dealt with at the camp.”",{"if":{"why":"refused"},"t":"The patrol rides out without you. Tom does not look back. You are left chained to a tree beside the supply horses, with one trooper to watch you. The patrol comes back in the afternoon. Nobody tells you what happened, and nobody looks at you."},{"if":{"why":"confessed"},"t":"The patrol turns back to the camp with you in irons at the end of the line."},"That night at the camp, Tom comes to the lean-to where you are chained. He is alone and he carries a water bag. He looks at the irons and then at the yard. “I did not see this,” he says. He puts the water down and walks away."],"choices":[{"label":"Wait for the punishment Hale decides on.","to":"end_irons"},{"label":"Check the irons now that Tom has gone.","to":"run"}],"real":[{"paras":["Troopers who refused orders or left the force were punished. Historian Jonathan Richards lists ten troopers who were shot for desertion, including two in 1863 and one in 1880."],"src":["burke"]},{"paras":["An inquiry in 1876 into the deaths of two troopers heard evidence that troopers were flogged. A witness said it was “a general occurrence in every Native Police camp”."],"src":["burke"]}]},"run":{"id":"run","title":"Leaving in the dark","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["The lock on the irons was not closed all the way. You slip your hands out and sit still until the camp is quiet.","The camp is dark. Your horse is in the yard and your carbine is in the sergeant’s hut. You know that men who leave the force are hunted.","You can go on foot with nothing that belongs to the force. That will be slow, and you will have little food. Or you can take your horse. That is faster, and the sergeant will count a stolen horse as another crime."],"choices":[{"label":"Go on foot, and take nothing that belongs to the force.","to":"end_leave"},{"label":"Take your horse.","to":"pursuit"}],"real":[{"paras":["In 1863 a trooper called Georgey petitioned the Governor of Queensland. He said he left the force after being badly treated by a camp sergeant. He said he knew that troopers who left had been shot. He wrote that he did not want to stay in the Native Police any longer."],"src":["georgey"]}]},"pursuit":{"id":"pursuit","title":"Dust behind you","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["On the third day you see dust behind you. Doyle and two troopers are following. They are men like you, doing a job they cannot refuse. They are good trackers, and you know it because you are one too.","You are tired, and the horse is tired. You know that Doyle will not stop."],"choices":[{"label":"Leave the horse and hide in the broken ground.","to":"end_leave"},{"label":"Stop and give yourself up before anyone is hurt.","to":"end_irons"}],"real":[{"paras":["Desertions are recorded for single troopers and for whole detachments. In Georgey’s case in 1863, a lieutenant and troopers arrested him at a station and took him back to the barracks in irons."],"src":["burke","georgey"]}]},"end_report":{"id":"end_report","title":"The report","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["You ride in with the others. It is almost light.","This story does not describe what happens next. The official report that evening uses one word for it, and that word is dispersal.","You ride back to the station. Mr Carver is told that the district will be quiet now. The report is short. It does not say how many people were there, who they were or what their names were. It does not mention you.","You stay in the force for years. You are sent north when the frontier moves. Nobody asks you about that morning, and you do not speak of it. You are fed, clothed and armed by the force, and you are still a long way from your own country."],"real":[{"paras":["Records of the time call this kind of event a “dispersal”. The records were written by officers and settlers. Very little is known from the troopers themselves, and almost nothing is known about what the troopers felt."],"src":["burke","nulungu"]},{"paras":["The Native Mounted Police were formally disbanded in 1904. In 1880 the Commissioner of Police wrote that detachments were being broken up, and that troopers were being sent to ordinary police stations as trackers."],"src":["nulungu","qsa"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You followed the order","summary":"Billy followed the order. The story does not describe the violence, because the people in the camp are the people this history belongs to, and their suffering should not be used for entertainment. Many troopers did follow orders, and many had very little choice."},"end_irons":{"id":"end_irons","title":"Irons","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["Hale decides your punishment the next morning. It is carried out in the yard in front of the other troopers. This story does not describe it.","You are not shot and you are not freed. You stay in the force under watch. For a long time Doyle does not let you carry a carbine.","Your refusal did not change what the force did next. The force moved north and west with the frontier, and new troopers were recruited. The others in the camp saw what happens to a man who refuses an order."],"real":[{"paras":["The records show that troopers were punished harshly. In the 1863 petition of Georgey, a trooper says he was put in irons and that he knew troopers who left had been shot. An 1876 inquiry heard evidence of floggings. Historian Jonathan Richards lists ten troopers who were shot for desertion."],"src":["georgey","burke"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You were punished","summary":"Billy was harmed for what he chose. The story shows that a trooper who refused or left had very few safe options, and that one man’s refusal did not stop the force."},"end_leave":{"id":"end_leave","title":"Leaving","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["You travel at night and hide in the day. You walk south, away from the camp, with nothing that belongs to the force.","After some days you reach a river you do not know. You are hungry. You do not know whether the people of this country will welcome a man who wore the uniform of the force that hunted them.","Some people give you water. Others turn away. At a station a long way from the camp, a stockman gives you a meal. He says there is work for food if you want it. You do not tell him where you have come from.","He asks your name. You give him the one from your own country."],"real":[{"paras":["Desertion was common. Many men left, and some were hunted. Georgey, who left in 1862, said he had not left sooner because he was afraid of being put into the bush far from his own people."],"src":["burke","georgey"]},{"paras":["People who survived the frontier years in Central Queensland were often used as cheap labour on stations, or lived on the edges of towns."],"src":["woor"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You left the force","summary":"Billy left. He was free of the camp but far from home, with no safe place to go. The story does not say whether he reached his country."},"end_stand":{"id":"end_stand","title":"The people at the spring","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["The old woman speaks to the old men. They talk for a long time and nobody tells you what they say. The young man who speaks English looks at you and says nothing.","At last she says you may come with them as far as the first water, and that after that they will decide. You take off your jacket. You leave the carbine on the ground beside the spring. The old woman looks at it and does not touch it.","Before light the camp is empty. The people go up into the ranges, and you go with them.","The story does not say what happens next. The patrol will find the jacket and the carbine, and Hale will know what you did. The force will look for you. Whether you are allowed to stay, and for how long, is a decision for the people. It is their country and it is their right to decide."],"real":[{"paras":["There is no known record of a trooper joining a group he had been sent against. The story marks it as invented. Records of the time were written by officers and settlers, so the choices made by Aboriginal people at the time are mostly not recorded."],"src":["burke"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You asked to go with the people","summary":"This ending is invented. No record is known of a trooper doing this. It is included to show that the local people made their own decisions, and that Billy had no right to expect to be welcomed."},"end_empty":{"id":"end_empty","title":"The empty camp","where":"Central Queensland · late 1870s","text":["Hale looks at you for a long time. Then he turns away. “The camp was empty,” he says, and he writes it in his report.","The patrol goes back to the camp. Nobody tells you whether the people at the spring got away, or whether they were the people who camped at the waterhole, or whether anyone from that camp had anything to do with the stockman.","You stay in the force. There will be other patrols, and you cannot lead all of them the wrong way. What you did was small. It was not nothing, and it was not enough.","Two years later the Commissioner of Police writes that some detachments are to be broken up. You do not know whether that will change anything for the people you meet."],"real":[{"paras":["On 1 June 1880 the Commissioner of Police wrote that several detachments had been broken up, and that the troopers had been placed at ordinary police stations as trackers, as part of “the gradual disembodying of the Native Police Force”."],"src":["qsa"]},{"paras":["The Native Mounted Police were formally disbanded in 1904."],"src":["nulungu"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You did one small thing","summary":"Billy did one small thing and kept quiet. The story shows that a small act could matter to the people who were spared, and that it did not change the system."}},"behind":{"intro":"This story is fiction built on real history. The real history is given in the panels called “Real history” and listed with its sources on the Sources page. Everything else is invented, and it is listed here.","sections":[{"kind":"made","h":"What is invented","list":["Billy: his camp name, his age, his home country and how he came to the force. The three ways of joining are based on methods the records describe, but Billy’s story is made up.","Sub-Inspector Hale, Camp Sergeant Doyle, Corporal Tom, Mr Carver and his overseer, Ridge Creek station and the camp on the creek. None of them is a real person or place.","The letter, the dead stockman, the missing cattle, the waterhole and the spring. No real event is meant. The story does not use any real massacre, and Billy does not take part in one.","The old woman, the young man who speaks English, and the people at the spring. They have no names, nation, language or customs in the story, because inventing these would be wrong. What they say and decide is invented.","All the dialogue, and everything Billy and the other characters think or feel. No trooper’s account of his own service is known, so nobody can say what troopers felt.","The scenes where Billy refuses, misleads the patrol, warns the camp, leaves the force or is pursued. These are shaped by what the records show, but no record is known of any of these particular acts.","The year (the late 1870s) and the place (“Central Queensland”). The place is made general on purpose."]},{"kind":"plain","h":"What the story leaves out on purpose","list":["The story does not describe the killing of Aboriginal people. The records show that it happened, and the real history panels say so plainly.","The story does not name real massacres or give them to a character. Real events such as these belong to the people and families who remember them.","The story does not describe Aboriginal ceremony, language, law or family connections, because these belong to the Traditional Owners.","The story uses the words of the time only where needed (“Native Police”, “trooper”, “dispersal”). It does not use the insulting words that appear in many historical sources."]},{"kind":"plain","h":"Fairness to the people in the story","list":["The troopers were Aboriginal men. Many had been taken from their own families and country, or had very little choice. Historians are careful about judging them. The story tries to show their choices and their limits.","The people who were hunted by the force are not shown as victims only. Aboriginal people in Central Queensland resisted the loss of their country, and the real history panels describe some of this."]}]},"discuss":[{"h":"Discuss","qs":[{"q":"The records use the word “dispersal”. What did it mean, and why did officers and officials use it?"},{"q":"List the three ways Billy could have come to the force. Which sources say that men were recruited in these ways? Why is it hard to say if someone “volunteered”?"},{"q":"Billy has choices, but are his choices equal to Hale’s? Who has the power in this story? Give examples from two different paths."},{"q":"Find the scene where the local people make their own decision. Who decides, and what does Billy’s news change? Why did the author not give them names or language?"},{"q":"Almost all the records of the Native Police were written by officers and settlers. What is missing? What can a historian do when a group’s voices are missing from the records?"},{"q":"Play at least three different paths. Which endings follow what the records show happened to troopers? Which ending is invented with no known record?"},{"q":"The story does not describe what happens at dawn. Is this the right choice? Does leaving it out hide the truth, or does it respect the people involved? Argue your view with evidence from the app."},{"q":"Who should decide how this history is told in schools? Why might the author ask Traditional Owners to review the app?"}]},{"h":"Write","qs":[{"q":"Write 200 words as a member of a parliamentary committee, such as the one that met in 1861, reading Hale’s report from the empty camp. What would you ask him? What would you want to know that the report does not say?"},{"q":"Choose one ending. Write a paragraph that explains what the ending tells us about the real history, using at least two of the real history panels."},{"q":"Research task: look at the Frontier Conflict database or the Colonial Frontier Massacres map for your region. Write down what the sources say and what they do not say. Take care with distressing material, and talk to your teacher first."}]}],"openingIntro":["You are Billy, a young trooper in the Native Mounted Police in Central Queensland in the late 1870s. The Native Police was a force of Aboriginal men led by white officers. It was used to “disperse” Aboriginal people from land that settlers wanted.","You will make choices. Each choice has a cost. There are five endings, and none of them is simple. Some paths end with you leaving, some with you being punished, and some with you following orders. Try more than one path.","Billy is an invented character. Behind the Story explains what is real and what is invented."]},"gold":{"id":"gold","repo":"qld-gold-rush-adventure","startChoices":[{"label":"Follow Sam, a young labourer in Brisbane in 1858","to":"s1"},{"label":"Follow Lin, a Chinese miner who leaves Charters Towers for the Palmer River in 1874","to":"l1"},{"label":"Follow a young Aboriginal person on the Palmer River in 1873","to":"a1"}],"startText":["Gold was found in Queensland many times between 1858 and 1877. Each time, thousands of people travelled to the new field. Each field was on Country where Aboriginal people already lived. Many of the diggers were British or Irish. Many were Chinese.","In this story you make the choices. You can follow one of three invented people. Each person lives through a different part of the gold rushes. When a story ends, you can go back and follow one of the others.","The story is invented. The facts are real. Boxes marked “Real history” show the facts and name their sources."],"warning":["This story is about gold rushes on the Country of Aboriginal people. It describes violence against Aboriginal people and racism against Chinese people. It does not describe the violence in detail.","Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this app names people who have died, and that the old records it draws on were written by Europeans.","The three main characters are invented. The Aboriginal character has no name and no named family group on purpose. Read “Behind the Story” to see what is real and what is invented.","This app has not yet been reviewed by Aboriginal community members. Review is being sought. Please tell the author if anything needs to change."],"review":null,"sources":{"slqCanoona":{"short":"SLQ blog on Canoona","t":"State Library of Queensland. “Gold Fever – The Canoona Delusion near Rockhampton” (blog).","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/gold-fever-canoona-delusion-near-rockhampton","note":"Gives the location of Canoona and quotes the Moreton Bay Courier of 28 July 1858, page 2 (on Trove)."},"qpCanoona":{"short":"Queensland Places, Canoona","t":"Queensland Places. “Canoona”. University of Queensland.","u":"https://queenslandplaces.com.au/canoona","note":"Gives the 15,000 estimate and says the field was not a “duffer” but could not support that many people."},"cqu":{"short":"CQU, Rockhampton history","t":"Central Queensland University. “An Overview of Rockhampton’s History” (PDF).","u":"https://library-resources.cqu.edu.au/cqcollection/manuscripts/short-manuscripts/rockhampton.pdf","note":"Dates for the proclamation of Rockhampton, the first land sales, and the return south of most of the people who came."},"csc":{"short":"Colonial Secretary letter, 1858","t":"Colonial Secretary’s Correspondence, transcribed by State Library of Queensland volunteers (FromThePage).","u":"https://fromthepage.com/slqld/colonial-secretary-s-correspondence/work-33054-013189","note":"An official’s 1858 letter about the Canoona field. I read only the part that is quoted in my summary. Robin should check the full letter."},"darumbal":{"short":"Darumbal Enterprises","t":"Darumbal Enterprises. “Darumbal History”.","u":"https://darumbal.com.au/darumbal-culture/darumbal-history/","note":"The Darumbal people are the Traditional Owners of the Rockhampton and Capricorn Coast area, including the Fitzroy River catchment."},"qsaGympie":{"short":"QSA, Gold at Gympie","t":"Queensland State Archives. “Gold at Gympie”. Stories from the Archives, 30 August 2021.","u":"https://blogs.archives.qld.gov.au/2021/08/30/gold-at-gympie/","note":"The 1866 recession, the £3,000 reward, James Nash’s find, how quickly the surface gold ran out, and the name Gympie."},"grmNash":{"short":"Gympie Regional Memories, Nash","t":"Gympie Regional Libraries. “Gold Discovered in Gympie: James Nash”. Gympie Regional Memories.","u":"https://gympieregionalmemories.com/2022/05/12/gold-discovered-in-gympie/","note":"Shows James Nash’s letter of 16 October 1867 (Queensland State Archives, item 1137542)."},"grmChinese":{"short":"Gympie Regional Memories, Chinese people","t":"Gympie Regional Libraries. “Chinese miners, gardeners and shopkeepers in Gympie”. Gympie Regional Memories.","u":"https://gympieregionalmemories.com/2022/03/11/chinese-miners-gardeners-shopkeepers-in-gympie/","note":"A library research project that found more than 260 named Chinese people on the Gympie goldfield."},"gfhsAb":{"short":"Gympie Family History Society, Aboriginal life","t":"Gympie Family History Society. “Aboriginal Life on the Gympie Goldfields” (Jody Allen, 12 May 2026).","u":"https://www.gfhs.com.au/aboriginal-life-on-the-gympie-goldfields/","note":"A local history society article. It is a secondary source, so Kabi Kabi custodians should check anything taken from it."},"gfhsChinese":{"short":"Gympie Family History Society, Ah Young","t":"Gympie Family History Society. “Ah Young: Chinese Immigrant and Mary Street Merchant”.","u":"https://www.gfhs.com.au/ah-young/","note":"Says Chinese miners worked ground left by European diggers. The article is partly a retelling, so I used only that general point."},"abs150":{"short":"ABS, 150 years of Queensland","t":"Australian Bureau of Statistics. “150 Years of Queensland’s Economic History: Key Dates, Facts and Figures”. Qld Stats, February 2009.","u":"https://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs@.nsf/Previousproducts/1318.3Feature%20Article14Feb%202009?issue=Feb+2009&num=&opendocument=&prodno=1318.3&tabname=Summary&view=","note":"Separation on 10 December 1859, the size of the Gympie, Palmer and Charters Towers rushes, and the 1877 laws."},"ctc":{"short":"Charters Towers Regional Council, history","t":"Charters Towers Regional Council. “History of our Region”.","u":"https://www.charterstowers.qld.gov.au/Council/Charters-towers-history/History-of-our-region","note":"The discovery in December 1871, the claim of 26 January 1872, the four mills of 1872 and the first millionaire miner in 1875."},"monJupiter":{"short":"Monument Australia, Jupiter Mosman","t":"Monument Australia. “Jupiter Mosman”.","u":"https://www.monumentaustralia.org/themes/people/indigenous/display/91079-jupiter-mosman","note":"Says Jupiter Mosman’s tribal name is unknown and that he found the gold-bearing quartz in December 1871."},"slqJupiter":{"short":"SLQ, Jupiter Mosman book","t":"State Library of Queensland Shop. “Jupiter Mosman: The Gold Discoverer”.","u":"https://shop.slq.qld.gov.au/products/9781922643216","note":"Says it took 30 years for his find to be acknowledged and a further 50 years for a monument."},"kirk84":{"short":"Kirkman, Palmer goldfield (1984)","t":"Noreen Kirkman. “The Palmer goldfield, 1873–1883”. Honours thesis, James Cook University, 1984.","u":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/57117/1/57117-kirkman-1984-thesis.pdf","note":"The most detailed study of the Palmer. It says its evidence about Chinese and Aboriginal people is almost entirely European in origin."},"kirk93":{"short":"Kirkman, “From Minority to Majority” (1993)","t":"Noreen Kirkman. “From Minority to Majority: Chinese on the Palmer River Gold-field, 1873–1876”. In Henry Reynolds (ed.), Race Relations in North Queensland, James Cook University, 1993.","u":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/26810/1/26810_Kirkman_in_Reynolds_Race_relations_1993_1.pdf","note":"Uses Queensland State Archives files and newspapers such as the Queenslander and the Cooktown Herald."},"kirk87":{"short":"Kirkman, “Chinese Miners on the Palmer” (1987)","t":"Noreen Kirkman. “Chinese Miners on the Palmer”. Journal of the Royal Historical Society of Queensland, vol. 13, no. 2, 1987.","u":"https://researchonline.jcu.edu.au/24925/1/s00855804_1987_13_2_49.pdf","note":"Numbers of Chinese miners in 1875 to 1877, the 1876 gold fields bill and the miners’ own accounts."},"parks":{"short":"Queensland Parks, Palmer Goldfield","t":"Queensland Parks and Forests. “Nature, culture and history: Palmer Goldfield Resources Reserve”.","u":"https://parks.qld.gov.au/parks/palmer-goldfield/about/culture","note":"The date of Mulligan’s find and the effects on Aboriginal people. The website blocked my reader, so I read this text through a search summary. Please check it on the page."},"hodes":{"short":"Hodes, 1877 law","t":"Jeremy Hodes. “John Douglas and restricting Chinese migration to Queensland in 1877”. Queensland History (blog), 2015.","u":"https://queenslandhistory.blogspot.com/2015/05/john-douglas-and-restricting-chinese.html","note":"The £10 tax, the ship limit and the refund rule."},"absYB":{"short":"ABS Year Book 1925","t":"Australian Bureau of Statistics. Year Book Australia, 1925: “Chinese in Australia”.","u":"https://www.abs.gov.au/ausstats/abs@.nsf/featurearticlesbytitle/4A6A63F3D85F7770CA2569DE00200137?OpenDocument=","note":"Queensland’s 1876 gold fields amendment, its approval in 1877, and the later changes of 1884 and 1888."},"aph":{"short":"Senate report on stolen wages, chapter 2","t":"Parliament of Australia, Senate Legal and Constitutional Affairs References Committee. “Unfinished business: Indigenous stolen wages”, Chapter 2, “Life under the protection acts”. 2006.","u":"https://www.aph.gov.au/parliamentary_business/committees/senate/legal_and_constitutional_affairs/completed_inquiries/2004-07/stolen_wages/report/c02","note":"Describes the Queensland Act of 1897, the permit and agreement rules for Aboriginal workers, and the control of wages."},"qsaSearch":{"short":"Queensland State Archives, Archives Search","t":"Queensland State Archives. Archives Search (online catalogue).","u":"https://www.archivessearch.qld.gov.au/","note":"For teachers: search here for gold fields warden records, mining warden court records and Colonial Secretary letters."},"trove":{"short":"Trove","t":"National Library of Australia. Trove: digitised newspapers.","u":"https://trove.nla.gov.au/","note":"For teachers: search for the Moreton Bay Courier (1858), the Nashville Times (1868), the Queenslander and the Cooktown Herald (1874 to 1877)."},"qhr":{"short":"Queensland Heritage Register","t":"Queensland Heritage Register (Department of the Environment, Tourism, Science and Innovation).","u":"https://apps.des.qld.gov.au/heritage-register/","note":"For teachers: search the register for the Palmer goldfield, Gympie and Charters Towers mining places. I did not use any register entry for a fact in the story, because the register pages would not load for my reader."},"qhatlas":{"short":"Queensland Historical Atlas","t":"Queensland Historical Atlas.","u":"https://www.qhatlas.com.au/","note":"For teachers: maps and essays on Queensland’s history. I did not use it for a fact in the story, because the site would not load for my reader."}},"sourcesIntro":"Every fact in a “Real history” box comes from one of these sources. Newspapers named in the sources can be read on Trove. Queensland State Archives holds the original government records. I read the sources listed here when I wrote this app.","imageNote":"All images are old photographs or drawings that are in the public domain. No images in this app were made by artificial intelligence. There are no images of Aboriginal people.","images":{"gympie":{"file":"images/mary-street-gympie-1868.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a wide dirt street with wooden buildings and groups of men standing in front of them.","caption":"Mary Street, Gympie, 1868. The buildings include Tattersall’s, the saleyards, the Bank of New South Wales and the Golden Age Hotel.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_15578_Mary_Street,_Gympie,_1868.jpg","people":false},"cooktown":{"file":"images/charlotte-street-cooktown-1874.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a street with a few wooden buildings and tents.","caption":"Charlotte Street, Cooktown, about 1874. Cooktown was the port for the Palmer River goldfield.","credit":"Unknown photographer, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Charlotte_Street,_Cooktown.jpg","people":false},"digger":{"file":"images/chinese-gold-digger-1860s.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a man in a wide hat with a long pole across his shoulders. A wooden box hangs from one end and a bucket from the other.","caption":"A Chinese gold digger with his mining tools hung from a pole, in the 1860s. Photograph by Richard Daintree.","credit":"Richard Daintree, copied by State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_2_116084_Chinese_gold_digger_starting_for_work,_ca._1860s.jpg","people":false},"towers":{"file":"images/charters-towers-bank-1875.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of a small wooden bank building with a sign, with a horse and cart in front.","caption":"The Bank of New South Wales at Charters Towers, about 1875. The branch opened in 1872.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_45675_Charters_Towers_branch_of_the_Bank_of_New_South_Wales,_ca._1875.jpg","people":false}},"nodes":{"s1":{"id":"s1","title":"News from Canoona","where":"Brisbane · August 1858","text":["You are Sam. You are twenty years old and you work as a labourer in Brisbane. Work is hard to find and the pay is low.","One morning a man at the wharf reads the newspaper out loud. It says that gold has been found at a place called Canoona, in the north. Within an hour everyone at the wharf is talking about it.","You have a little money saved. A ship could take you north. The fare is high, and you might find nothing."],"choices":[{"label":"Buy a ticket on a ship to the north","to":"s2"},{"label":"Stay in Brisbane and keep your work","to":"s3","set":{"canoona":"stayed"}}],"real":[{"h":"Canoona, 1858","paras":["On 28 July 1858 the Moreton Bay Courier reported that gold had been found at Canoona. Canoona is about 40 kilometres north-west of Rockhampton, on the Fitzroy River. An estimated 15,000 people were at Canoona between September and December 1858.","At this time Queensland was still part of New South Wales. It became a separate colony on 10 December 1859. The land around Rockhampton and the Fitzroy River is the Country of the Darumbal people."],"src":["slqCanoona","qpCanoona","abs150","darumbal"]}]},"s2":{"id":"s2","title":"Canoona","where":"Canoona, near Rockhampton · October 1858","text":["The ship lands you at Rockhampton, a small settlement on the Fitzroy River. Many people have arrived before you. The riverbank is crowded with tents and with men asking how to get to the diggings.","You walk to Canoona with a group of strangers. The diggings are crowded. Most men dig for days and find no gold, or only a little. Some say the newspapers tricked them.","You have to decide how to use your money and your time."],"choices":[{"label":"Keep digging at Canoona","to":"s2b"},{"label":"Go back to Rockhampton and look for paid work","to":"s3","set":{"canoona":"rocky"}},{"label":"Take a ship back south","to":"s3","set":{"canoona":"returned"}}],"real":[{"h":"A rush that did not last","paras":["An official wrote in 1858 that the ground taken up by the first diggers could give work to only a very small number of men. Queensland Places says the field was not a fraud, but it could not support anything like 15,000 people.","Most of the thousands who came were returned south by the governments in Sydney and Melbourne. A few hundred stayed and started businesses. Rockhampton was proclaimed a town on 25 October 1858, and its first land sales were on 17 and 18 November 1858."],"src":["csc","qpCanoona","cqu"]}]},"s2b":{"id":"s2b","title":"Still digging","where":"Canoona · November 1858","text":["You stay. You and two other men share a claim. Week after week you wash the dirt in the creek. You find a few small grains of gold. They are worth less than the food you buy.","Your money is nearly gone. Each morning, more men pack up and leave. By the end of the month you can see that the field will not make you rich."],"choices":[{"label":"Leave and look for paid work in Rockhampton","to":"s3","set":{"canoona":"rocky"}},{"label":"Leave on a ship back south","to":"s3","set":{"canoona":"returned"}}],"real":[{"h":"What the records say","paras":["An official’s letter said there had been no extension of the gold ground that gave any promise, beyond the patch taken up by the first diggers. This is one reason the rush is remembered as a failure."],"src":["csc","qpCanoona"]}]},"s3":{"id":"s3","title":"The Mary River","where":"Brisbane and Gympie · October 1867","img":"gympie","text":[{"if":{"canoona":"stayed"},"t":"Nine years have passed since the news from Canoona. You stayed in Brisbane and kept working as a labourer."},{"if":{"canoona":"rocky"},"t":"Nine years have passed since Canoona. You found paid work in Rockhampton for a while, and later came back to Brisbane."},{"if":{"canoona":"returned"},"t":"Nine years have passed since Canoona. You came back to Brisbane with empty pockets and worked wherever you could."},"It is October 1867. Banks have closed. Many men have lost their work. Then news arrives from the north. A man named James Nash has found gold near the Mary River, in the Wide Bay district.","You travel there. Within weeks thousands of people have arrived. The field is called Nashville. Along the creeks you see Chinese miners working claims that other diggers have left. Aboriginal families camp near the diggings. Some of the men guide travellers and carry supplies for the diggers.","One afternoon a group of European diggers shouts at two Chinese miners to leave a gully. The Chinese miners keep working and say nothing. You are standing close by."],"choices":[{"label":"Say nothing and keep working","to":"s3b","set":{"gully":"silent"}},{"label":"Tell the diggers to leave the Chinese miners alone","to":"s3b","set":{"gully":"spoke"}}],"real":[{"h":"Gympie, 1867","paras":["Early in 1866 Queensland was in a recession made worse by a long drought. A bank had closed and unemployed men were protesting in the streets of Brisbane. The government offered a £3,000 reward for a new goldfield. In August 1867 James Nash found gold near the Mary River. On 16 October 1867 he wrote to claim the reward. At the height of the rush there were about 16,000 diggers on the field. The town was first called Nashville and later Gympie, from the Gubbi Gubbi word gimpi gimpi, the name of a stinging tree.","Gympie is on Kabi Kabi (Gubbi Gubbi) Country. A local history society says that an Aboriginal camp formed at the edge of the town in the late 1860s, and that Aboriginal people worked as guides, carriers and timber workers. It also says they were often paid in rations or goods, not wages. Chinese people came to Gympie too. Library records name more than 260 Chinese people on the goldfield, and say they were later gardeners, shopkeepers and miners. The same records say Europeans harassed Chinese people but that the courts did not accept it."],"src":["qsaGympie","grmNash","abs150","gfhsAb","grmChinese","gfhsChinese"]}]},"s3b":{"id":"s3b","title":"Under the ground","where":"Gympie · 1868 to 1871","text":[{"if":{"gully":"silent"},"t":"You try to forget the afternoon in the gully, but you do not. You see the same Chinese miners at their claim every week."},{"if":{"gully":"spoke"},"t":"Some of the diggers stop talking to you after the afternoon in the gully. A few say you were right."},"The gold that lies near the surface is mined out within about a year. Many diggers leave. The mine owners start to dig shafts into the rock. You take wages at a deep mine. The work is hot, dark and tiring.","In early 1872 a miner brings news from far in the north. Gold has been found in the rock at a place called Charters Towers."],"choices":[{"label":"Stay at Gympie and keep your job underground","to":"e1"},{"label":"Go north to Charters Towers","to":"s4"}],"real":[{"h":"Gympie after the surface gold","paras":["The Queensland State Archives says the surface gold was mined out within a year, so the miners went underground. The field was worked for about 60 years, and production was highest between 1900 and 1910. Gympie became known as the town that saved Queensland, because its gold helped end the recession."],"src":["qsaGympie","abs150"]}]},"s4":{"id":"s4","title":"Charters Towers","where":"Charters Towers · 1872","img":"towers","text":["You travel north by ship and then overland. When you arrive there are tents and a few huts below a hill. Men are digging out lumps of quartz, a hard white rock. The gold is inside the rock. It must be crushed by machines before it can be washed.","You hear how the field was found. A party of prospectors was camped near the hill. A horse boy named Jupiter was with them, and he was the first to find the gold-bearing rock.","You need to decide how to work. Wages are steady but small. A claim could bring much more, or nothing."],"choices":[{"label":"Take wages at a crushing mill","to":"e2","set":{"towers":"wages"}},{"label":"Join three partners and peg a claim on a reef","to":"e2","set":{"towers":"claim"}}],"real":[{"h":"Charters Towers, 1871 and 1872","paras":["In December 1871 a party of prospectors found gold near a hill at what became Charters Towers. The party was Hugh Mosman, George Clarke, a man named Fraser, and an Aboriginal horse boy named Jupiter, who is credited with finding the gold-bearing quartz. Jupiter’s own name and his people are not known. A book about him says that it took 30 years for his find to be acknowledged and a further 50 years for a monument to be put up.","Hugh Mosman applied for a protection area for the claim on 26 January 1872, and the place was named after the Gold Commissioner, W. S. E. M. Charters. By the end of September 1872 four crushing mills were working. In 1875 Frank Stubley became the first millionaire miner on the field. The area is the Country of the Gudjal people and others, according to the regional council."],"src":["ctc","monJupiter","slqJupiter"]}]},"e1":{"id":"e1","title":"Underground at Gympie","where":"Gympie · after 1872","text":["You stay. You work in the mines for many years. The town grows around the shafts. There are shops, hotels and churches. Chinese neighbours grow vegetables and run stores. At the edge of town, the Aboriginal camp is smaller each year.","This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible. Many people lived like this."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["Gympie’s deep mines kept the town going for decades. The field produced gold for about 60 years. Library records show Chinese gardeners and shopkeepers in the town, and floods sometimes destroyed their gardens.","The local history society says a measles outbreak in 1894 struck the Aboriginal camp at Gympie, and that in 1898 the surviving Aboriginal people from Gympie were removed to the Bogimbah Creek reserve on Fraser Island. This should be checked with Kabi Kabi custodians before it is repeated."],"src":["abs150","qsaGympie","grmChinese","gfhsAb"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"e2":{"id":"e2","title":"A reef miner at Charters Towers","where":"Charters Towers · after 1872","text":[{"if":{"towers":"wages"},"t":"You take wages at the mill and save a little each week. Over the years you move from the mill to a mine. It is steady work."},{"if":{"towers":"claim"},"t":"You and your partners peg a claim. You spend months digging a shaft and paying for the stone to be crushed. Some weeks the stone has gold. Other weeks it does not."},"The town grows. It gets a bank, a newspaper, hotels and a school. The gold is deep in the rock, so mining needs machines, money and many workers.","This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["Charters Towers became Queensland’s premier goldfield and for a time its second largest city. By 1910 the mining had slowed.","Jupiter’s part in the discovery was not widely acknowledged for about 30 years. His own name and people are not recorded."],"src":["abs150","ctc","slqJupiter","monJupiter"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"l1":{"id":"l1","title":"News of the Palmer","where":"Charters Towers · May 1874","img":"digger","text":["You are Lin. You are twenty-eight years old. You came from the province of Guangdong in southern China some years ago. You came to earn money to send to your family. You work a claim at Charters Towers with two friends.","In May 1874 news arrives from far in the north. Payable gold has been found on the Palmer River. Other Chinese miners and families are already leaving the Etheridge and Charters Towers fields to go there.","The Palmer is a very long way off, and few supplies are for sale along the way. You can leave now with a group, or wait and save more money first."],"choices":[{"label":"Leave now with a group","to":"l2","set":{"palmer":"early"}},{"label":"Wait and save more money first","to":"l2","set":{"palmer":"late"}}],"real":[{"h":"The Palmer River rush","paras":["Gold was first reported on the Palmer River by the Hann expedition in 1872. James Venture Mulligan and six companions found payable alluvial gold on 29 June 1873. Cooktown was settled from October 1873 as the port. In the 1871 census there were 3,305 Chinese people in Queensland. By late May 1874, Chinese miners and their families were heading to the Palmer from the Etheridge and Charters Towers fields.","Many Chinese men went to the goldfields to earn money for their families. Many of those who came directly from China were sponsored by Chinese merchants and had to repay a debt before they could mine for themselves."],"src":["kirk93","kirk84","parks"]}]},"l2":{"id":"l2","title":"On the Palmer","where":"Palmer River · 1874 and 1875","img":"cooktown","text":[{"if":{"palmer":"early"},"t":"You arrive in August 1874. There are about a thousand Chinese people on the field. There is a temple, led by a Chinese doctor."},{"if":{"palmer":"late"},"t":"You arrive in early 1875. Steamers carrying Chinese passengers from overseas have begun to arrive at Cooktown. The first, the Victoria, arrived on 30 January 1875 with 200 passengers."},"You work ground that European diggers left when they rushed to new finds. The gold is still there. You work slowly and carefully, one square of ground at a time.","Some European diggers are angry. They say Chinese miners push up the price of flour. Others buy your vegetables and rice and say nothing. At Sandy Creek a notice is nailed to a tree. It says any Chinese man found higher up the creek will be hanged."],"choices":[{"label":"Keep mining, even where some Europeans say you are not wanted","to":"l3","set":{"lin":"mine"}},{"label":"Plant a garden and sell vegetables and rice to the camps","to":"l3","set":{"lin":"garden"}}],"real":[{"h":"Chinese miners on the Palmer","paras":["By the end of August 1874 there were about a thousand Chinese on the Palmer, with a temple led by Dr King Chong. By the end of 1874 there were about 1,500, almost 40 per cent of the field’s people. In 1875 steamers brought thousands more from China. About 9,000 Chinese were on the Palmer in July 1875.","The Chinese worked ground that European miners had left, and many Europeans blamed them for high prices. Historian Noreen Kirkman says it would have been clear to most Europeans that Chinese people were the chief suppliers of vegetables and stores on the field. In May 1875 a notice at Sandy Creek threatened to hang any Chinese man found higher up the creek. Kirkman says it was a sign of anger, not a plan. The large riots that had taken place at Lambing Flat in New South Wales in 1860 and 1861 did not happen on the Palmer."],"src":["kirk93","kirk87"]}]},"l3":{"id":"l3","title":"The Palmer changes","where":"Palmer River · 1876","text":["In March 1876 news arrives of gold on the Hodgkinson River. Thousands of European diggers leave the Palmer. Almost the whole field is left to the Chinese miners. Steamers now arrive every week from Hong Kong with new men.","In May many Europeans return and find Chinese miners on their old claims. Some try to force the miners off. The warden, Philip Sellheim, punishes some Europeans who try to jump Chinese claims.","You know that the Palmer is the Country of Aboriginal people. Many have been driven from the river by the diggers and by the Native Police. You hear of attacks and killings. You keep your camp close to others.",{"if":{"lin":"mine"},"t":"Your claim gives you a little gold each week."},{"if":{"lin":"garden"},"t":"Your garden gives you a steady income, and you buy a share in a claim with your savings."}],"choices":[{"label":"Stay on the Palmer through 1877","to":"l4"},{"label":"Leave now with your gold for Cooktown and a ship home","to":"e4"}],"real":[{"h":"The field and the law","paras":["After the Hodgkinson rush began in March 1876, the Palmer was largely left to Chinese miners. Warden Sellheim had to punish a few Europeans who tried to jump Chinese claims. By 1876 there were about 17,000 Chinese and 1,400 Europeans on the field.","In 1876 the Queensland parliament passed a bill that made Chinese and other Asian miners pay a miner’s right of £3, six times the fee for European miners, and business licences of £10. The governor sent the bill to London before approving it. It became law in 1877."],"src":["kirk93","kirk87","abs150","absYB"]}]},"l4":{"id":"l4","title":"The new law","where":"Palmer River and Cooktown · 1877","text":["In the first five months of 1877 thousands more Chinese miners arrive. There are far too many for the ground. Most men are finding very little gold. Many are sick and some are very poor. You meet men who came with great hopes and now want to go home.","Then you hear of a new law in Brisbane. Every Chinese person arriving by ship will be taxed £10. The tax will be paid by the ship’s captain. Only a few Chinese passengers will be allowed on each ship. The tax will be paid back to people who leave within three years and have not broken the law.","You can go home, or you can stay."],"choices":[{"label":"Go home with what you have saved","to":"e4"},{"label":"Stay in Queensland","to":"e3"}],"real":[{"h":"Queensland, 1877","paras":["A total of 7,708 Chinese miners arrived on the field in the first five months of 1877, taking the population to about 18,000. By the end of May the average earnings were estimated at only three pennyweights. A man named Taam Sze Pui, who arrived then, wrote that many earlier arrivals were poor and ill and were thinking of leaving.","In 1877 Queensland passed a law that put a £10 tax on each Chinese person arriving by ship, allowed one Chinese passenger for every 10 tons of a ship’s size, and refunded the tax to anyone who left within three years without breaking the law or costing the colony money. A later law banned Asian and African miners from new goldfields for three years after a discovery. Sources give the date as 1877 or 1878."],"src":["kirk87","hodes","abs150","absYB"]}]},"e3":{"id":"e3","title":"A life in Queensland","where":"Queensland · after 1877","text":["You stay. You leave the Palmer when the gold runs low and you find work in a town. You grow vegetables, work as a cook or run a small store. You send money to your family when you can.","You see few new arrivals from China. The laws make it very costly for more to come. This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["Only about 500 new Chinese people arrived in the colony between mid-1877 and 1881. In 1884 Queensland raised the tax to £30. In 1888, after a conference of the colonies, it passed a new law like those in the southern colonies.","Chinese communities continued in Queensland towns. At Gympie, library records show Chinese gardeners, shopkeepers and miners who took part in community life."],"src":["hodes","absYB","grmChinese"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"e4":{"id":"e4","title":"Home with savings","where":"Cooktown · 1877","text":["You leave Cooktown on a steamer for Hong Kong. You have some gold, but less than you hoped. You are going home to your family.","This ending is invented. It shows one life that was possible."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["Many Chinese miners took gold home. In October 1875 two steamers carried 14,000 ounces of gold to Hong Kong, including an 80-ounce nugget. When large numbers of Chinese miners returned to China with gold, there was a public outcry in Queensland. The Palmer gave about 100 tonnes of gold in all.","Many of the men who came in 1877 found very little gold. Not everyone went home with savings."],"src":["kirk93","abs150","kirk87"]}],"end":true,"kind":""},"a1":{"id":"a1","title":"Watching the strangers","where":"The Palmer River country · September 1873","text":["You are a young person of the Aboriginal people whose Country includes part of the Palmer River. This person is invented. No name and no family group is given, on purpose. See Behind the Story.","You know this river and its waterholes. In the dry season the waterholes hold fish. People come to the river to fish and to camp. The river is the main source of food for your family.","Your people watch strangers from a distance. If strangers come too close, you give a warning. In the last few months a small party of strangers has come and gone. Now a larger party arrives with horses and tools. They dig in the riverbed. The water turns muddy."],"choices":[{"label":"Stay near the river and keep watching","to":"a2"},{"label":"Move your family to higher country, away from the diggers","to":"a3"}],"real":[{"h":"The Palmer River, 1873","paras":["James Venture Mulligan and six companions found payable gold on 29 June 1873. The rush began in September 1873. Historian Noreen Kirkman writes that the Aboriginal way of watching and warning strangers did not survive the arrival of the first miners. The rivers were the main source of food for Aboriginal people, and the miners worked in the same rivers for gold. This is a main reason that conflict began.","The Queensland Parks service says that game was killed or driven away, and that waterholes that had been clear became muddy."],"src":["kirk84","kirk93","parks"]}]},"a2":{"id":"a2","title":"The camp near the river","where":"The Palmer River · late September 1873","text":["You camp a short walk from the diggers. From the high bank you can see them at work. One afternoon several of the young diggers walk up to a neighbouring camp, about a quarter of a mile from their work. They fire revolver shots. People run into the scrub.","When you go back to the camp the diggers have turned over the camp and taken tools. They have broken spears.","In the next weeks, some of the diggers are chased back to their camp. Some people say the first attack was a mistake. Others say it is the start of a war."],"choices":[{"label":"Fight to keep the waterhole","to":"a4","set":{"path":"fight"}},{"label":"Leave the river and go to higher country","to":"a3"}],"real":[{"h":"What the records say","paras":["On 27 September 1873 a group of miners forcibly removed Aboriginal people from a camp near where they were working. According to Mulligan, the attack was not provoked, and it marked the start of the conflict. In the first month of the rush the Aboriginal people returned and frightened miners out of the gullies two or three times. A later raid by ten armed miners on a camp of thirty Aboriginal people at Fish Creek was recorded, but the record did not say what happened to the Aboriginal people."],"src":["kirk84"]}]},"a3":{"id":"a3","title":"The high country","where":"The Palmer River country · 1874 to 1877","text":["In the high country there are fewer diggers. There is also less water. In the dry season your family finds less food than before. Animals for hunting are scarce.","The diggers now have horses and cattle. The people who work on the river will not leave. More arrive every month. The Native Police ride out on patrols. You hear that they have broken up other camps.","In dry years like 1877 there is not enough food. Some of your people take horses and cattle that the diggers have left on the river."],"choices":[{"label":"Stay in the high country","to":"a4","set":{"path":"high"}},{"label":"Return to the river when fewer diggers are there","to":"a4","set":{"path":"return"}}],"real":[{"h":"What the records say","paras":["In 1877 the Queenslander wrote that Native Police officers said most Aboriginal people on the Palmer were half starved, because the Europeans had taken the watercourses. The paper said that Aboriginal people killed horses and cattle out of necessity.","The Native Police were detachments of Aboriginal troopers led by European officers. They were sent to protect the diggers and to carry out “dispersals”, the word used for breaking up Aboriginal camps. Kirkman says that little is known about the way the force worked, and that it kept up patrols and was called in for reprisals after specific incidents."],"src":["kirk84"]}]},"a4":{"id":"a4","title":"After the rush","where":"The Palmer River country · about 1880","text":[{"if":{"path":"fight"},"t":"Many people were hurt in the fighting at the river. Families moved away to places they felt were safer."},{"if":{"path":"high"},"t":"You kept to the high country for most of these years. Your family is smaller now."},{"if":{"path":"return"},"t":"You went back to the river when you could. You were not always welcome. Your family is smaller now."},"By about 1880 the great rush is over. Many of the Chinese and European diggers have gone. The Native Police patrols are fewer. The river is still your Country, but life on it has changed.","Most of the records of this time were written by Europeans. They did not write down what the people of the river thought."],"choices":[{"label":"Teach your children about the river and the waterholes","to":"e5","set":{"end":"teach"}},{"label":"Keep away from the strangers who remain","to":"e5","set":{"end":"away"}}],"real":[{"h":"After the rush","paras":["From 1877 the numbers of Native Police were reduced across the colony. By 1880 the number was less than half that of 1876. Kirkman writes that by the end of the decade the catastrophic disruption of Aboriginal society and the decline in the numbers of Europeans and Chinese had reduced the level of conflict.","Kirkman also writes that the evidence about Aboriginal people on the Palmer is almost entirely European in origin, and is often hostile or at best indifferent."],"src":["kirk84"]}]},"e5":{"id":"e5","title":"What the records leave out","where":"The Palmer River country · after 1880","text":[{"if":{"end":"teach"},"t":"You teach your children about the river. You show them where the waterholes are and where the camps were."},{"if":{"end":"away"},"t":"You keep away from the strangers who remain. You teach your children to do the same."},"This story is invented. The people of the Palmer River are real. The Aboriginal peoples of this region survived, and their descendants live in Queensland today. This story cannot say what they thought and felt. Only they can say that."],"real":[{"h":"What happened in real history","paras":["Historian Noreen Kirkman describes violence on the Palmer as a European initiative, and a decade of brutality. Almost all the records were written by Europeans.","In 1897 Queensland passed the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act. Under it, an Aboriginal worker could be employed only under a permit from a protector, and employers had to sign an agreement. Together with later protection laws, it gave the government wide control over the lives of Aboriginal people."],"src":["kirk84","kirk93","aph"]}],"end":true,"kind":""}},"behind":{"sections":[{"kind":"made","h":"What is invented","p":["All of the story text is invented. The facts are in the boxes marked “Real history”."],"list":["Sam, the people he meets, his job, his savings, his choices and his gold claim are invented.","Lin, Lin’s two friends, Lin’s home town, Lin’s age and every choice Lin makes are invented.","The young Aboriginal person on the Palmer is invented. I have deliberately given no name and no family group, because I do not know who the people of that part of the river were and I do not want to invent a name or customs.","The scene of the diggers shouting at the Chinese miners at Gympie is invented. It stands for harassment that library records say happened on that field.","The scene at the Palmer waterhole and the camp is invented. It is modelled on the attack of 27 September 1873 recorded by Noreen Kirkman.","Every ending is invented. They show lives that were possible, not lives that were recorded.","No real person speaks in the story. Real people are named only in the fact boxes."]},{"kind":"real","h":"What is real","list":["The dates and places of the rushes: Canoona in 1858, Gympie in 1867, Charters Towers in 1871 and 1872, and the Palmer River in 1873 to 1877.","The estimated 15,000 people at Canoona, the proclamation of Rockhampton on 25 October 1858, and the return south of most of the people who had come.","The recession of 1866, James Nash’s find and letter of 16 October 1867, and the name of Gympie.","The finding of gold-bearing rock at Charters Towers by a party that included an Aboriginal horse boy named Jupiter.","The numbers of Chinese people on the Palmer in 1874 to 1877, the notice at Sandy Creek, the Hodgkinson rush, and the Queensland laws of 1876 to 1877.","The attack on an Aboriginal camp on 27 September 1873, the fear and hunger that followed, and the Native Police patrols."]},{"kind":"real","h":"What I could not find, or what historians do not agree on","list":["I did not find a source that describes what happened to Darumbal people at Canoona in 1858.","Who “discovered” gold is not simple. The Queensland State Archives says gold was noticed near Gympie in 1851 by John Carne Bidwill. At the Palmer, the Hann expedition reported gold in 1872 before Mulligan’s find in 1873. At Charters Towers, Jupiter is credited with the find but was not credited for about 30 years.","The name of the third man in the Charters Towers party is written as John Fraser in one source and James Fraser in another. I used only “Fraser”.","The numbers of Aboriginal people killed on the Palmer in the 1870s are not known. Kirkman writes that little is known about how the Native Police worked.","Some information about Aboriginal people at Gympie comes from a local history society article. It needs to be checked by Kabi Kabi custodians."]},{"kind":"care","h":"Care with Aboriginal history, and cultural review","p":["Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander readers are advised that this app names people who have died, including Jupiter Mosman and James Nash, and that it describes violence against Aboriginal people.","The records of the 1870s often used insulting words about Aboriginal and Chinese people. I have not quoted those words. I used the words “Aboriginal people”, “Chinese miners” and “Native Police”. The Native Police were Aboriginal troopers, led by European officers, who were used to break up Aboriginal camps.","The Aboriginal path has been written without names or customs of any people. The author wants to ask Traditional Owners and Aboriginal community members to review this app before it is used widely. The people to ask include the Darumbal people for Canoona, Kabi Kabi (Gubbi Gubbi) custodians for Gympie, Gudjal and other Traditional Owner groups for Charters Towers, and Traditional Owners of the Palmer River country. Until that review is done, please treat this app as a draft."]},{"kind":"real","h":"Words in this story","p":["“Rush” means a sudden movement of many people to a new goldfield. “Alluvial gold” is gold in creek gravel. “Reef gold” is gold in rock. A “warden” was an official who ran a goldfield. A “miner’s right” was a licence to mine. “Dispersal” was the word used for breaking up an Aboriginal camp by force."]}]},"discuss":[{"h":"Understand","qs":[{"q":"Why did thousands of people go to Canoona in 1858, and why did most of them leave?"},{"q":"Why did the Queensland government offer a reward for gold in 1866, and why was Gympie called “the town that saved Queensland”?"},{"q":"Why did Chinese miners work ground that European miners had left? What did European miners say about them, and what do the records show?"}]},{"h":"Think and discuss","qs":[{"q":"List three ways that the gold rushes changed life for Aboriginal people in the places in this story. Which source do you use for each?"},{"q":"Noreen Kirkman writes that the evidence about Aboriginal people on the Palmer is almost entirely European in origin. How does this change what we can know? Which voices are missing?"},{"q":"The Palmer River goldfield was unusual. By 1876 there were about 17,000 Chinese and 1,400 Europeans. Why did the colony’s government make new laws in 1876 and 1877? Were they fair?"},{"q":"Jupiter, the Aboriginal horse boy, is credited with finding gold at Charters Towers, but it took about 30 years for this to be acknowledged. Why might that be? What does it mean that his own name is not known?"},{"q":"The story puts invented scenes next to real facts. What can an invented story show that a source cannot? What are the risks?"}]},{"h":"Write","qs":[{"q":"Write a diary entry for Sam, for Lin or for the young Aboriginal person. Use only facts from the Real history boxes. Mark any sentence that is invented."},{"q":"Write a paragraph for or against this statement: “The £10 tax on Chinese arrivals in 1877 was a fair way to control immigration.” Use two facts."},{"q":"What would you want to ask Traditional Owners before telling this story to a class? List three questions."}]}]},"sugar":{"id":"sugar","repo":"sugar-story-adventure","startChoices":null,"warning":["This resource may contain names and images of deceased Aboriginal, Torres Strait Islander and South Sea Islander people.","It is about the Queensland sugar industry, the labour trade that brought Pacific Islanders to Queensland (often called blackbirding), deaths from disease, the deportations of 1906 to 1908, and the dispossession of Aboriginal people. It does not show violence in detail, but it does describe it. Some old photographs show people who have died. You can stop at any time. If the story upsets you, talk to a teacher, a family member or someone you trust."],"review":"Review status: this resource has not yet been reviewed by Australian South Sea Islander community groups or by Aboriginal Traditional Owners. The author is not a member of these communities. It should be reviewed before it is used widely, for example by the Queensland United Australian South Sea Islander Council (QUASSIC), local Australian South Sea Islander groups, and the Traditional Owners of the Mackay district, including the Yuibera people. Teachers, please seek advice from your local Australian South Sea Islander and Aboriginal communities before you use it in class.","sources":{"quassic1":{"t":"QUASSIC (Queensland United Australian South Sea Islander Council). Historical Information Sheet No 1: Defining Slavery, Indenture, and Blackbirding.","u":"https://www.quassic.org.au/no-1-defining-slavery-indenture-and-blackbirding","short":"QUASSIC sheet 1"},"quassic6":{"t":"QUASSIC. Historical Information Sheet No 6: Mortality and Pacific Islander Migrants in Colonial Queensland.","u":"https://www.quassic.org.au/no-6-mortality-and-pacific-islander-migrants-in-colonial-queensland","short":"QUASSIC sheet 6"},"quassic7":{"t":"QUASSIC. Historical Information Sheet No 7: Incidents of Abuse of Pacific Islanders during the Recruiting Process to Queensland.","u":"https://www.quassic.org.au/no-7-incidents-of-abuse-of-pacific-islanders-during-the-recruiting-process-to-queensland","short":"QUASSIC sheet 7"},"naaSSI":{"t":"National Archives of Australia. Fact sheet: South Sea Islanders.","u":"https://www.naa.gov.au/help-your-research/fact-sheets/south-sea-islanders","short":"NAA fact sheet"},"naaPILA":{"t":"National Archives of Australia. Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 (learning resource).","u":"https://www.naa.gov.au/students-and-teachers/learning-resources/learning-resource-themes/society-and-culture/migration-and-multiculturalism/pacific-island-labourers-act-1901","short":"NAA, Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901"},"peo":{"t":"Parliamentary Education Office. Pacific Islanders Labourers Act 1901 (with a General Certificate of Exemption dated 4 May 1907).","u":"http://peo.gov.au/understand-our-parliament/history-of-parliament/history-milestones/australian-parliament-history-timeline/events/pacific-islanders-labourers-act-1901","short":"Parliamentary Education Office"},"qsa":{"t":"Queensland State Archives. Australian South Sea Islanders (collection guide and indexes, 1867–1948).","u":"https://www.qld.gov.au/recreation/arts/heritage/archives/collection/assi","short":"Queensland State Archives"},"docdem":{"t":"Museum of Australian Democracy, Documenting Democracy. Pacific Island Labourers Act Amendment Act 1884 (Qld). Original held by Queensland State Archives, SRS 1296/1/17.","u":"https://www.foundingdocs.gov.au/item-sdid-53.html","short":"Documenting Democracy, 1884 Act"},"nma":{"t":"National Museum of Australia. Defining Moments: Islander labourers.","u":"https://www.nma.gov.au/defining-moments/resources/islander-labourers","short":"National Museum of Australia"},"slq":{"t":"State Library of Queensland, John Oxley Library blog (2013). A White Australia (a review of the 1901 booklet “A White Australia: the Kanaka Labor Question”).","u":"https://www.slq.qld.gov.au/blog/white-australia","short":"State Library of Queensland blog"},"moore2000":{"t":"Moore, C. (2000). “Good-bye, Queensland, good-bye, White Australia; Good-bye Christians”: Australia’s South Sea Islander Community and Deportation, 1901–1908. The New Federalist, 4, 22–29.","u":"https://www.multiculturalaustralia.edu.au/doc/moore_ssi_deport.pdf","short":"Moore (2000)"},"moore2015":{"t":"Moore, C. (2015). The Pacific Islanders’ Fund and the Misappropriation of the Wages of Deceased Pacific Islanders by the Queensland Government. Australian Journal of Politics and History, 61(1), 1–18.","u":"https://www.assipj.com.au/southsea/wp-content/uploads/misappropriation-of-wages.pdf","short":"Moore (2015)"},"uq":{"t":"University of Queensland. Unskilled labour migration into Queensland (Research Week 2010 paper on the Polynesian Labourers Act 1868 (Qld)).","u":"https://law.uq.edu.au/files/4284/Slavery-or-Salvation-Research-Week-2010.pdf","short":"University of Queensland paper"},"trove1885":{"t":"The Labour Trade. Newspaper report of 6 May 1885 on the voyage of the Hopeful, via Trove (National Library of Australia), article 174162604.","u":"http://trove.nla.gov.au/newspaper/rendition/nla.news-article174162604.txt","short":"Trove, The Labour Trade, 6 May 1885"},"mackayCouncil":{"t":"Mackay Regional Council (2008). Mackay history lift-out: “A new port in Queensland” and “Sugar boom”.","u":"https://www.mackay.qld.gov.au/__data/assets/pdf_file/0005/19490/library_12_page_lift-out.pdf","short":"Mackay Regional Council history lift-out"},"mackayTimeline":{"t":"Mackay Family History and Historical Society. Mackay Timeline: a chronological history of Mackay events.","u":"http://mackayhistory.org/research/timeline/timeline.html","short":"Mackay history timeline"},"alexandra":{"t":"Mackay history research. Alexandra Sugar Mill, 1868–1884.","u":"http://www.mackayhistory.org/research/sugar_mills/alexandra.html","short":"Alexandra Sugar Mill, Mackay history"},"pioneerMill":{"t":"Mackay history research. Pioneer Sugar Mill, 1867–1882 (John Spiller planted the first cane in the district on 1 June 1865).","u":"https://mackayhistory.org/research/sugar_mills/pioneer.html","short":"Pioneer Plantation, Mackay history"},"sarina":{"t":"Queensland Government. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community histories: Sarina (covers the Mackay district).","u":"https://www.qld.gov.au/firstnations/cultural-awareness-heritage-arts/community-histories/community-histories-s-t/community-histories-sarina","short":"Queensland Government, Sarina community history"},"yarrabah":{"t":"Queensland Government. Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander community histories: Yarrabah (covers the Cairns district).","u":"https://www.qld.gov.au/firstnations/cultural-awareness-heritage-arts/community-histories/community-histories-u-y/community-histories-yarrabah","short":"Queensland Government, Yarrabah community history"},"senate":{"t":"Parliament of Australia, Senate Legal and Constitutional References Committee (2006). Unfinished business: Indigenous stolen wages, Chapter 2: Life under the protection Acts.","u":"https://www.aph.gov.au/parliamentary_business/committees/senate/legal_and_constitutional_affairs/completed_inquiries/2004-07/stolen_wages/report/c02","short":"Senate stolen wages report, ch. 2"},"mcneill":{"t":"McNeill, H. and Marmo, M. (2023). Past–present differential inclusion: Australia’s targeted deportation of Pacific Islanders, 1901 to 2021. International Journal for Crime, Justice and Social Democracy, 12(1), 42–56.","u":"https://doi.org/10.5204/ijcjsd.2743","short":"McNeill and Marmo (2023)"},"wall":{"t":"Queensland Heritage Register, entry 602230: South Sea Islander Wall, Mon Repos, Bundaberg Region (listed 1 October 2001).","u":"https://apps.des.qld.gov.au/heritage-register/detail/?id=602230","short":"Queensland Heritage Register 602230"},"jcu":{"t":"James Cook University Library Archives. Pioneer Sugar Mill Archive (Burdekin district).","u":"https://libserver.jcu.edu.au/specials/Archives/pioneer.html","short":"JCU Library, Pioneer Sugar Mill Archive"},"hambledon":{"t":"Canetrains wiki. Hambledon Mill (Edmonton, near Cairns). Cites the Australian Dictionary of Biography entry for Thomas Swallow.","u":"https://canetrains.net/wiki/Hambledon_Mill","short":"Canetrains, Hambledon Mill"},"caneQld":{"t":"Canetrains wiki. Queensland (history of the sugar industry, including the Sugar Works Guarantee Act 1893).","u":"https://canetrains.net/wiki/Queensland","short":"Canetrains, Queensland"},"bundabergCouncil":{"t":"Bundaberg Regional Council. Acknowledgement of Country (Taribelang Bunda, Gooreng Gooreng, Gurang and Bailai Peoples).","u":"https://www.ourbundabergregion.com.au/acknowledgement-of-country","short":"Bundaberg Regional Council"},"photoBundaberg":{"t":"Photograph: South Sea Islanders on the deck of a ship arriving at Bundaberg, 1895 (R. J. Cottell, from Pugh’s Almanac 1895, p. 149). State Library of Queensland negative 16954, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain).","u":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_50564_South_Sea_Islanders_on_the_deck_of_a_ship_arriving_at_Bundaberg,_1895.jpg","short":"Photograph, Bundaberg 1895"},"photoHambledon":{"t":"Photograph: South Sea Islander workers hoeing weeds on a sugar cane plantation at Hambledon, Queensland, about 1891. State Library of Queensland (photographer unidentified; no known copyright restrictions), via Wikimedia Commons.","u":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_South_Sea_Islander_workers_hoeing_weeds_on_a_sugar_cane_plantation_in_Hambledon,_Queensland,_ca._1891_(9205456322).jpg","short":"Photograph, Hambledon about 1891"}},"sourcesIntro":null,"imageNote":"There are two images in this app. Both are old photographs, in the public domain in Australia or with no known copyright restrictions. They show people who have died. The people in them are not named in the captions. You can choose whether to look at them. There are no AI images and no drawings made for this app.","images":{"bundaberg":{"file":"images/bundaberg-1895.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of many South Sea Islanders standing close together on the deck of a sailing ship at Bundaberg in 1895. Ropes and masts cross the picture, and one white man stands at the right edge.","caption":"South Sea Islanders on the deck of a ship arriving at Bundaberg, 1895. Photograph by R. J. Cottell, from Pugh’s Almanac 1895, State Library of Queensland.","credit":"","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_50564_South_Sea_Islanders_on_the_deck_of_a_ship_arriving_at_Bundaberg,_1895.jpg","people":true,"summary":"a photograph from 1895 (shows people who have died)"},"hambledon":{"file":"images/hambledon-1891.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of about a dozen men standing among rows of young sugar cane at Hambledon near Cairns, about 1891. Some hold hoes. Hills are behind them and the words “Hoeing weeds in young cane” are printed on the photograph.","caption":"South Sea Islander workers hoeing weeds on a sugar cane plantation at Hambledon, Queensland, about 1891. State Library of Queensland.","credit":"","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Australian_South_Sea_Islander_workers_hoeing_weeds_on_a_sugar_cane_plantation_in_Hambledon,_Queensland,_ca._1891_(9205456322).jpg","people":true,"summary":"a photograph from about 1891 (shows people who have died)"},"mill":{"file":"images/pioneer-mill-mackay-1885.jpg","alt":"A black and white photograph of the Pioneer Sugar Mill at Mackay, with wooden mill buildings, two brick chimneys, a large heap of cut cane in front and a group of workers standing near the buildings.","caption":"Pioneer Sugar Mill at Mackay, about 1885. The people in the photograph are not named.","credit":"John Oxley Library, State Library of Queensland (public domain)","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:StateLibQld_1_13930_Pioneer_Sugar_Mill_at_Mackay,_1880s.jpg","people":true,"summary":"the Pioneer Sugar Mill at Mackay in the 1880s (it shows people who have died)"}},"nodes":{"how":{"id":"how","title":"Before the ship","where":"An island in the New Hebrides · 1884","set":{"cont":"yes","land":"no","abo":"no","trade":"goods","voice":"none","join":"chose","eng":"no","hut":"given","left":"late"},"text":["This story is set in 1884. You are about seventeen years old. You live in a village on an island in the New Hebrides. Today the New Hebrides are part of the country of Vanuatu. The story does not name your island or your language. People from many islands and many language groups were part of this history, and one story cannot speak for all of them.","Ships from Queensland come to the islands to recruit young people for plantation work. The work is for three years. A white captain and a white recruiter run each ship, and a Government Agent travels on board as well. A ship is now anchored near your village.","How do you come to be on board?"],"choices":[{"label":"You choose to go. Young men from near your village went on a ship and came home with goods. You want to earn goods too.","to":"ship","set":{"join":"chose"}},{"label":"You are told a story. A man tells you that the work will last one or two moons. You do not know that it will last three years.","to":"ship","set":{"join":"told"}},{"label":"You are taken. Men pull you into a boat. You did not agree to go.","to":"ship","set":{"join":"taken"}}],"real":[{"paras":["The Queensland Government has records of 62,475 indenture contracts for Pacific Islanders between 1863 and 1904. Many men signed more than once, so there were probably about 50,000 individual people. About 95 per cent were adolescent or young adult males. Only about 5 per cent were women."],"src":["quassic1"]},{"paras":["QUASSIC says historians estimate that about 10 to 15 per cent of recruits were kidnapped as the law defined it. It says most kidnapping happened in the first ten or so years in any area, and that some continued until the 1890s. It also says that many young men joined freely, sometimes more than once. It adds that the whole process took people from their communities and bound them with legal contracts they did not understand."],"src":["quassic1"]},{"paras":["A newspaper report of 6 May 1885 on the voyage of the Hopeful describes men pulled on board and told that they were to go and work in a white man’s country “for one or two moons”."],"src":["trove1885"]},{"paras":["There were 870 labour-recruiting voyages between Queensland and the Pacific Islands from 1863 to 1904."],"src":["quassic7"]}]},"ship":{"id":"ship","title":"The voyage","where":"At sea · 1884","text":["The ship is crowded. Men and boys from several islands are on board, and some speak languages you do not know. The ship is at sea for many days.",{"if":{"join":"chose"},"t":"You are afraid, but you chose to come. You listen while the other men talk about what they hope to buy with their pay."},{"if":{"join":"told"},"t":"Someone tells you the ship is going to Queensland. You ask when the ship will bring you home. No one gives you an answer that you understand."},{"if":{"join":"taken"},"t":"You do not know where the ship is going. You try to talk to the other men, and some of them are afraid too."},"The Government Agent is a white man who has a book. The law says he must check that the people on the ship came willingly and that the ship follows the rules. He speaks to you through an interpreter. In this story the interpreter is an islander who works for the ship. What do you say?"],"choices":[{"label":"Tell him the truth: you were told one or two moons, and you did not know it was three years.","to":"landing","set":{"voice":"truth"},"if":{"join":"told"}},{"label":"Tell him the truth: you were taken, and you did not agree to come.","to":"landing","set":{"voice":"truth"},"if":{"join":"taken"}},{"label":"Say that you chose to come.","to":"landing","set":{"voice":"said"}},{"label":"Ask him how long the work will last.","to":"landing","set":{"voice":"asked"}},{"label":"Say nothing.","to":"landing","set":{"voice":"none"}}],"real":[{"paras":["The Polynesian Labourers Act 1868 was the first Queensland Act made for the labour trade. It required employers to apply for approval, banned recruiters from kidnapping, set minimum standards for the voyage and set agreements of three years."],"src":["uq","moore2015"]},{"paras":["The Pacific Island Labourers Act 1880 replaced the 1868 Act. It made forced recruiting illegal and required inspectors, called Government Agents, on the ships. The National Museum of Australia says that agents were not always careful and some could be bribed by the crew."],"src":["nma","moore2015"]},{"paras":["In 1871 the Government Agent on the Jason tried to stop abuses. The captain had him handcuffed and chained to a ring-bolt for more than three weeks."],"src":["quassic7"]},{"paras":["The 1884 voyages of the Hopeful went to islands east of New Guinea. The crew were tried in Brisbane in late 1884. A Royal Commission said the cruise was “one long record of deceit, cruel treachery, deliberate kidnapping and cold-blooded murder”. The men were found guilty. The death sentences were later commuted (reduced)."],"src":["quassic7"]}]},"landing":{"id":"landing","title":"Mackay, 1884","where":"Mackay · 1884","text":[{"if":{"voice":"truth"},"t":"The Government Agent writes in his book. You do not know what he writes. The ship does not turn back."},{"if":{"voice":"said"},"t":"The Government Agent writes in his book. The ship goes on."},{"if":{"voice":"asked"},"t":"The Government Agent says that the work will last three years. Now you know how long it is. The ship goes on."},{"if":{"voice":"none"},"t":"The Government Agent goes on to the next person. The ship goes on."},"The ship arrives at the port of Mackay. A doctor looks at the new arrivals. Then you are walked with a group to a plantation on the Pioneer River.","The overseer reads a list. The name beside yours is not your name. He calls you Harry. That is the name you will be called here.","Your agreement is for three years. You will be paid £6 a year, paid in two parts, and given rations, clothes and a hut. You make a mark on a paper. It is not in your language, and you cannot read it.","The overseer shows you the huts. You can ask to share a hut with people from your own area, or you can take the hut that you are given."],"choices":[{"label":"Ask to share a hut with people from your own area.","to":"work","set":{"hut":"own"}},{"label":"Take the hut you are given.","to":"work","set":{"hut":"given"}}],"real":[{"paras":["Pastoralists settled the Mackay district from 1862. John Spiller planted the first sugar cane in the district on 1 June 1865. About 70 Pacific Islanders arrived on the ship Prima Donna on 13 May 1867 to start work as indentured labourers on plantations around Mackay."],"src":["pioneerMill","mackayTimeline"]},{"paras":["The Alexandra mill began crushing on 15 September 1868. By 1875, 23 sugar mills had been built in the district, before a cane disease damaged the crop. A second boom from 1879 to 1884 added 12 more mills."],"src":["alexandra","mackayCouncil"]},{"paras":["The 1884 Amendment Act limited newly arrived labourers to field work in tropical agriculture. They could not work in sugar mills, the pastoral industry or the maritime industry."],"src":["docdem"]},{"paras":["The standard agreement was for three years, at £6 a year, with rations, clothing, basic accommodation, medical care and a return passage."],"src":["slq","moore2015"]},{"paras":["The words “Kanaka” and “blackbirding” appear in the records and in books. “Kanaka” is now an offensive word, and the story does not use it."],"src":["quassic1"]}]},"work":{"id":"work","title":"The first months","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · 1884","text":["You start work clearing land and planting cane. The land is cleared by hand. Later you hoe weeds, strip dead leaves from the cane and cut ripe cane with a cane knife. The cut cane goes to the mill on carts.",{"if":{"hut":"own"},"t":"You share a hut with people from your own area. At night you can talk in your own language."},{"if":{"hut":"given"},"t":"You share a hut with men from three different islands. You learn a few words of each other’s languages. You also learn English words used on the plantation."},"There are very few women on the plantation. Most of the workers are men and boys. The work is hard and the days are long. When the wet season comes, you work in the rain.","The overseer and the cart drivers speak English. You can start to learn their words, or you can keep mostly to your own language."],"choices":[{"label":"Learn English words from the overseer and the cart drivers.","to":"sick","set":{"eng":"yes"}},{"label":"Keep mostly to your own language and learn English more slowly.","to":"sick","set":{"eng":"no"}}],"real":[{"paras":["The work in the cane fields included land clearing, holing, planting, weeding, trashing (stripping dead leaves), cutting, ratooning (growing a new crop from the old roots) and carting cane. Islanders worked barefoot in wet, hot fields."],"src":["slq"]},{"paras":["The hundreds of languages of Melanesia made it hard for islanders from different places to talk. Between 1880 and 1900 a Pidgin English developed on the plantations and became a common language. Later it helped Islanders act together."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["Only about 5 per cent of indentured Pacific Islanders were women, so most plantation workforces were almost all male."],"src":["quassic1"]}]},"sick":{"id":"sick","title":"Sickness","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · 1884","text":["In your second month a man in the next hut begins to cough and shake. He comes from another island and he is also a new arrival. He has a fever. Men on the plantation say that new arrivals fall ill more often than men who have been here longer.","The overseer tells him to keep working. What do you do?"],"choices":[{"label":"Ask the overseer to send him to the hospital.","to":"wages","set":{"help":"asked"}},{"label":"Stay with him at night and share your water and food.","to":"wages","set":{"help":"stayed"}},{"label":"Say nothing. You are afraid of being punished.","to":"wages","set":{"help":"silent"}}],"real":[{"paras":["Government records show that more than 14,564 Islander labourers died in Queensland between 1868 and 1906. The real figure is probably closer to 15,000. That is about 24 per cent of the total number of indenture contracts. The main killers were tuberculosis, pneumonia, bronchitis, pleurisy, dysentery, measles and chicken pox."],"src":["quassic6"]},{"paras":["The death rate in the first year of an indenture was about 81 per 1,000. The death rate for Europeans of all ages in Queensland was about 15 per 1,000. The death rate for Islanders in Queensland reached its highest point in 1884 at 147 per 1,000."],"src":["quassic6"]},{"paras":["New arrivals suffered most. QUASSIC says that the longer an Islander lived in Queensland, the better the chance of a normal life span. It says that exposure to new diseases was the main cause of death, and that employers and the government can be blamed for keeping the system going for forty years after the death rate was known."],"src":["quassic6"]},{"paras":["The Queensland Government set up the Pacific Islanders’ Fund in 1885. It held the unpaid wages of Islanders who died. Clive Moore found that the government seldom returned the full wages to the families of the dead, and used the money for other costs of the labour trade."],"src":["moore2015"]}]},"wages":{"id":"wages","title":"Pay and goods","where":"Mackay district, Queensland","text":[{"if":{"help":"asked"},"t":"The overseer sends the sick man to the hospital after two days. You do not see him for three weeks. He comes back thinner, and he is alive."},{"if":{"help":"stayed"},"t":"The sick man gets better slowly in the hut. You are tired for several days because you slept little. Then you catch a cough yourself, and it passes after a week."},{"if":{"help":"silent"},"t":"The sick man falls down at work. The overseer sends him to the hospital. He is away for weeks and then he comes back. You wish you had spoken sooner."},"Over that year several men on the plantation die of fever, chest illness and dysentery. This is not rare. No one tells you where they are buried.","At the end of six months you are paid for the first time. The pay for six months is £3. Some of it is in cash and some of it is in goods from the plantation store. What do you do with it?"],"choices":[{"label":"Buy goods, such as cloth, a knife and tobacco, to take home one day.","to":"creek","set":{"trade":"goods"}},{"label":"Save as much as you can in an account.","to":"creek","set":{"trade":"saved"}}],"real":[{"paras":["The standard pay for a first-indenture labourer was £6 a year from the 1860s. After 1880 it was paid every six months. It did not change for about forty years, while wages in the general workforce rose. Moore writes that it is a myth that Islanders were not paid, but the pay was far lower than the pay of European workers, and many Islanders received only part of their pay in cash."],"src":["moore2015"]},{"paras":["Not every person was paid. The activist Faith Bandler said that her father and his relatives were paid nothing. She said she thought of it as a form of slavery."],"src":["nma"]},{"paras":["QUASSIC explains that, in law, indenture was not the same as slavery. Slavery was a lifelong legal status. Indenture was a contract for a set time. But it also says that Australian South Sea Islanders call themselves the descendants of slaves, and that a deep sense of injustice cannot be answered by only denying that they were slaves."],"src":["quassic1"]}]},"creek":{"id":"creek","title":"The creek","where":"Mackay district, Queensland","text":["On a Sunday you walk to the creek at the edge of the plantation. An Aboriginal man is fishing downstream. He is about your age or a little older. He sees you. Neither of you speaks.","This is Aboriginal country. In this district it includes the country of the Yuibera people. Settlers took up land here in the 1860s, and there was fighting and killing in the 1860s and 1870s. The man at the creek knows this history and so do his family. You do not know it yet.","The overseer has told you that the Aboriginal people from the creek cannot be trusted. What do you do?"],"choices":[{"label":"Nod to him and wait to see if he wants to speak.","to":"contract_end","set":{"abo":"contact"}},{"label":"Walk away and say nothing to anyone.","to":"contract_end","set":{"abo":"no"}},{"label":"Tell the overseer that you saw a man at the creek.","to":"contract_end","set":{"abo":"told"}}],"real":[{"paras":["Aboriginal people resisted the settlers who took their land. Widespread frontier violence happened across the Mackay district in the 1860s and 1870s. Native Police barracks were built at Marlborough in 1866 and at Bloomsbury, just north of Mackay, in 1868. The Queensland Government history says that the Native Police were important in crushing Aboriginal resistance."],"src":["sarina"]},{"paras":["In 1871 the government set aside 14,080 acres near Cape Palmerstone and Homebush as an Aboriginal reserve. The area was reduced to 10,000 acres by 1878. In 1879 funding was cut, the school closed and 90 per cent of the reserve was opened for settlement. The residents had to move away. Some Aboriginal men had been hired out to local employers as labourers while the reserve operated."],"src":["sarina"]},{"paras":["The people whose country includes the Mackay district have strong connections to it today. The Queensland Government history names the Yuibera, Wiri, Barada Barna Kabalbara and Yetimarla, Birri-Gubba and Kungalburra peoples for the nearby Sarina area."],"src":["sarina"]}]},"contract_end":{"id":"contract_end","title":"The end of the agreement","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · 1887","text":[{"if":{"abo":"contact"},"t":"The man at the creek nods back. He asks you a few words in English. You find that you each know some English and some words of the plantation Pidgin. He says he is there to fish, and he goes on fishing. Over the next two years you see him and some of his family at the creek on some Sundays. You hear that people from his family cut cane on farms upriver in some seasons. He and the people he knows have been moved off much of this land. You do not become close friends, but you start to know each other."},{"if":{"abo":"no"},"t":"You walk on. You do not know whether the man at the creek watches you go. You see him again at the creek once or twice, and each time you nod and each time he nods back."},{"if":{"abo":"told"},"t":"The overseer says that the Aboriginal people are on the plantation’s land without permission and that he will deal with it. A few weeks later the camp near the creek has been moved on. You do not know how or where to. No one tells you, and you do not ask."},"It is 1887. Your three years are over. You are about twenty years old. The law says that the planter must pay for your passage home if you ask. You can also sign a new agreement and stay in Queensland.",{"if":{"trade":"goods"},"t":"You have goods from the store that you could take home."},{"if":{"trade":"saved"},"t":"You have savings in an account."},"Many men stay on a second agreement. Many go home. You have to decide."],"choices":[{"label":"Take the passage home.","to":"home"},{"label":"Sign a new agreement and stay in Queensland.","to":"central"}],"real":[{"paras":["The 1884 Amendment Act sorted Islanders into three groups. First-indenture labourers were limited to field work in tropical agriculture. Time-expired labourers, who had finished one agreement, were also limited in some cases, but others could work anywhere in the colony. “Ticket-holders”, who had arrived before 1 September 1879, were bound only by the ordinary laws of Queensland."],"src":["docdem"]},{"paras":["Many people re-engaged without leaving Queensland. Moore writes that they became skilled in choosing employers and negotiating wages. By 1895 time-expired Islanders were 65 per cent of the Melanesians in the Mackay district."],"src":["moore2000","quassic6"]},{"paras":["Most people on a first agreement were meant to serve three years and go home. The labour trade was a circular migration, not designed for settling in Queensland."],"src":["quassic6"]}]},"home":{"id":"home","title":"Home","where":"Home island · 1887 to 1889","text":["You go home on a ship that carries people back to their islands. The ship lands you at your own island.",{"if":{"trade":"goods"},"t":"You bring cloth, a knife and other goods. Your family and neighbours welcome you and ask many questions about Queensland. Some of the goods are shared out."},{"if":{"trade":"saved"},"t":"You bring the money you saved. Before the ship leaves you spend some of it on goods. Your family and neighbours welcome you and ask many questions about Queensland."},"Life at home has changed. Ships have come more often. Some young people have gone to Queensland, and some have gone to Fiji and Samoa. You are not the same as when you left, and some people see you differently.","Two years pass. In 1889 a recruiting ship comes again. A recruiter you do not know says that Queensland will pay wages again. Some of your friends talk about going. What do you do?"],"choices":[{"label":"Stay at home.","to":"end_home"},{"label":"Sign again and go back to Queensland.","to":"return","set":{"cont":"no"}}],"real":[{"paras":["Re-enlistments from the islands began in the 1860s. In 1897, 230 of 934 recruits (24.6 per cent) had already served an indenture. By the 1890s about one-quarter of new arrivals had worked in Queensland, Fiji or Samoa."],"src":["quassic6","moore2000"]},{"paras":["Recruiting for Queensland came mainly from the New Hebrides and the Solomon Islands. Around 80 islands in all were part of the trade, including islands that are now part of Vanuatu, Solomon Islands, New Caledonia, Fiji, Kiribati and Papua New Guinea."],"src":["nma"]},{"paras":["The 1880 Act put a bond on ship owners so that they delivered passengers and their property back to the correct island at the end of their agreements."],"src":["moore2015"]}]},"return":{"id":"return","title":"Back in Queensland","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · 1889","text":["You sign in 1889 and sail again to Queensland. The agreement is for three years. You are placed on a plantation near Mackay.","The talk among the planters is about the law. In 1885 Parliament passed a law to stop recruiting after 1890. Many planters and politicians want it changed. People on the plantation say that the ships may stop coming.","When your agreement ends in 1892, you will need to decide what to do next. For now you work through the years on the plantation."],"choices":[{"label":"Work out the agreement on the plantation.","to":"central"}],"real":[{"paras":["In 1885 Queensland passed a law meant to end recruiting. In 1892 Parliament repealed it and the labour trade continued until 1904. A Royal Commission said that sugar could not be grown at a profit for export “without the employment for hand work in the field of a class of labor cheaper and more suitable for the work than white labor”."],"src":["slq","moore2000"]},{"paras":["Sources describe the 1885 to 1892 changes in different ways. The National Museum of Australia says that a ban in 1891 was postponed. The story only says what the State Library of Queensland blog says."],"src":["nma","slq"]}]},"central":{"id":"central","title":"The central mill","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · the 1890s","text":[{"if":{"cont":"yes"},"t":"You stayed in Queensland and signed new agreements. You have lived in the Mackay district since 1884, and you have worked for more than one employer."},{"if":{"cont":"no"},"t":"You came back in 1889. By now you know the district and you know how to bargain for your pay."},"It is the 1890s. The plantation system is changing. Mills are now built to crush cane from many small farms, not only from one large plantation. The Queensland Government lends money to groups of farmers to build them.","Most of the new farmers are white families who want to work their own land. Parliament and many voters want the sugar industry to use white labour in the future. In 1896 a regulation says that a time-expired Islander must either return home within a month of an agreement ending or sign a new agreement.",{"if":{"abo":"contact"},"t":"You still see people from the creek family now and then. A woman from that family works on the farm next to yours. You have known her for years."},"You earn wages. You can save toward a small block of land, or you can keep working for wages and not buy land. Some Islanders have bought land."],"choices":[{"label":"Save to buy a small block of land near the mill.","to":"protector","set":{"land":"yes"},"if":{"abo":"contact"}},{"label":"Keep working for wages and do not buy land.","to":"protector","set":{"land":"no"},"if":{"abo":"contact"}},{"label":"Save to buy a small block of land near the mill.","to":"law1901","set":{"land":"yes"},"if":{"abo":"no"}},{"label":"Keep working for wages and do not buy land.","to":"law1901","set":{"land":"no"},"if":{"abo":"no"}},{"label":"Save to buy a small block of land near the mill.","to":"law1901","set":{"land":"yes"},"if":{"abo":"told"}},{"label":"Keep working for wages and do not buy land.","to":"law1901","set":{"land":"no"},"if":{"abo":"told"}}],"real":[{"paras":["The first government-supported central mills in the Mackay district opened at North Eton in 1888 and at Racecourse in 1889. After that, the plantation system declined faster. The Sugar Works Guarantee Act 1893 allowed the Queensland Government to lend money for central mills. Farmers formed companies and mortgaged their land to secure the loans."],"src":["mackayCouncil","caneQld"]},{"paras":["Moore writes that Queensland aimed for a new structure of small farms owned by white Australians, using their families as labour and supplying co-operative central mills paid for at first from the Treasury. Islander labour was by then no longer cheap, and Islanders were no longer short-contract servants."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["In 1896 Queensland made a regulation that every time-expired Islander must, within one month after an agreement ended, either return to the islands or sign a new agreement with an employer."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["Many Islanders had bank accounts, possessions or property by the 1890s. The ticket-holders and the Islanders who re-engaged made up 40 to 60 per cent of the Islander population from the mid-1880s."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["Sugar grew in other districts too. At Bundaberg the South Sea Islander Wall at Mon Repos was built about 1884, probably by Islander labour, to clear stony land for cane. It is about 1,580 metres long and is listed on the Queensland Heritage Register. In the Burdekin, the Pioneer partnership began in 1883 and had five plantations and a mill by 1884. Low rainfall was a problem, and the partners started irrigation schemes. Near Cairns, the Hambledon mill began crushing in 1883, and in 1888 it employed 176 South Sea Islanders, 29 Chinese, 6 Javanese and 32 Europeans."],"src":["wall","jcu","hambledon"]},{"paras":["Each sugar district is on Aboriginal country. For example, Bundaberg Regional Council acknowledges the Taribelang Bunda, Gooreng Gooreng, Gurang and Bailai Peoples as Traditional Owners. The Queensland Government history of the Cairns area records that Native Police “dispersals” killed many Yidindji and Djabugay people, and that Yarrabah mission was founded in 1892, where Aboriginal people and some South Sea Islanders were relocated by government."],"src":["bundabergCouncil","yarrabah"]}]},"protector":{"id":"protector","title":"The Protector","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · 1897","text":["By 1897 you know the woman from the creek family well. You and she decide to live together. It is her decision as much as yours. Her family knows you.","That year the Queensland Parliament passes a new law about Aboriginal people. In every district there is a Protector, who is often a police officer. He can say where Aboriginal people live and work, and he can send them to a reserve or a mission. An employer must have a permit to employ an Aboriginal worker.","One morning the Protector comes to the farm. He asks her name and who she lives with. He says that he may send her to a reserve. What do you do?"],"choices":[{"label":"Ask her what she wants to do, and follow what she decides.","to":"law1901","set":{"abo":"partner","prot":"asked"}},{"label":"Speak to the Protector yourself and say that she is your wife.","to":"law1901","set":{"abo":"partner","prot":"spoke"}},{"label":"Say nothing. You are afraid of attracting attention.","to":"law1901","set":{"abo":"partner","prot":"silent"}}],"real":[{"paras":["In 1897 Queensland passed the Aboriginals Protection and Restriction of the Sale of Opium Act. Under it and later Acts, local Protectors controlled Aboriginal labour and savings, and could remove people to reserves and missions. An Aboriginal worker could only be employed under a permit from a Protector, and an employer had to make an agreement of no more than 12 months."],"src":["senate","sarina"]},{"paras":["From 1898 to 1967 there were 72 documented removals of Aboriginal people from the Mackay district. Most were removed to Yarrabah, Barambah (now Cherbourg), Woorabinda and Palm Island."],"src":["sarina"]},{"paras":["In 1901 the Act was amended to set a minimum wage for Aboriginal workers under permit. For most workers it was five shillings, which the Senate committee says was less than one-eighth of the white wage. From 1904 wages could be paid directly to the Protector."],"src":["senate"]}]},"law1901":{"id":"law1901","title":"A new law","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · 1901","text":[{"if":{"prot":"asked"},"t":"Years ago the Protector asked her questions, and she answered them herself. She said that she wanted to stay near her family and her country. He wrote it down. For now she stays, and she keeps working under a permit."},{"if":{"prot":"spoke"},"t":"Years ago, when you spoke to the Protector, he looked at you and then spoke to her. He asked her the same questions again. He wrote in his book and left. For now she stays, and she keeps working under a permit."},{"if":{"prot":"silent"},"t":"Years ago she answered the Protector’s questions herself. She told you later that it would have helped to have you stand beside her. For now she stays, and she keeps working under a permit."},"In 1901 the Australian colonies join to form the Commonwealth of Australia. The new Parliament passes laws to make a “White Australia”. One of them is the Pacific Island Labourers Act. The Governor-General agrees to it on 17 December 1901.","The Act says that no new labourers can be licensed after 31 March 1904. It says that from the end of 1906 the government can deport Pacific Islanders. The only people who are exempt are those who came to Queensland before 1 September 1879, people who work in ships’ crews, and people who are given a certificate of exemption.",{"if":{"eng":"yes"},"t":"You can read the news in English. You read it twice and then explain it to a friend."},{"if":{"eng":"no"},"t":"A friend who reads English tells you what the news says. You ask him to say it again."},{"if":{"abo":"partner"},"t":"You think about her and her family. You do not know what the Act will mean for the two of you."},"There are about 10,000 Pacific Islanders in Queensland and northern New South Wales. In November 1901 some Islanders in Mackay start the Pacific Islanders’ Association. Its purpose is to oppose deportation. What do you do?"],"choices":[{"label":"Join the Association and add your name to petitions.","to":"petition","set":{"joined":"yes"}},{"label":"Do not join. Keep working and saving.","to":"petition","set":{"joined":"no"}},{"label":"Decide to leave before the deadline, using your savings for a passage.","to":"end_early","set":{"left":"early"}}],"real":[{"paras":["The Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 was one of the first laws passed by the new Commonwealth Parliament. It was part of a package that made up the White Australia policy, with the Immigration Restriction Act 1901. Assent was given on 17 December 1901."],"src":["naaPILA","moore2000"]},{"paras":["Under the Act, labourers could enter under licence until 31 March 1904, in limited numbers. In 1902 entry was limited to three-quarters of the number who had left in 1901. In 1903 it was limited to half of the 1902 departures. No labour agreements were valid after the end of 1906, and after that date any person suspected of being a Pacific Islander had to prove that they were not."],"src":["naaPILA"]},{"paras":["About 10,000 Pacific Islanders lived in Queensland and northern New South Wales when the Act passed. About 700 were ticket-holders who were exempt. In 1901, 839 Islanders returned to the islands and another 1,678 arrived."],"src":["moore2000","naaPILA"]},{"paras":["The Pacific Islanders’ Association was formed in Mackay in November 1901. In August 1902 the Colonial Office received a petition to King Edward VII from about 3,000 Islanders. About 1,800 of the signatures were collected by Fanny Nicol, a missionary at Bundaberg. The King was advised to refuse it, but it embarrassed the Australian Government."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["Queensland’s Premier, Robert Philp, wrote in November 1901 that deporting people who had come as legal immigrants would cause “great inhumanity and injustice”."],"src":["moore2000"]}]},"petition":{"id":"petition","title":"The Association and the Royal Commission","where":"Mackay district, Queensland · 1904 to 1906","text":["Years pass. Recruiting ends in 1904. At the end of 1904 there are still 7,879 Islanders in Queensland, with the largest groups at Bundaberg, Mackay and Ingham. You are about thirty-seven.",{"if":{"joined":"yes"},"t":"You go to the Association’s meetings in Mackay. Members come from different islands, and they do not always agree about what to do. You listen, and you add your name to the petitions."},{"if":{"joined":"no"},"t":"You hear about the Association’s meetings from people at work and at church, but you do not go. You are saving money and you want to keep your job."},"In April 1906 a Queensland Royal Commission collects evidence about what the Act will do to the sugar industry and to Islanders. A delegation from the Association gives it a petition in Mackay. The Commission reports on 30 June. On 12 October 1906 an amendment to the Act becomes law. It adds new groups who are allowed to stay.","The groups are: people who have lived in Australia continuously since 31 December 1886; people who are aged or infirm; people whose children go to state schools; owners of freehold land; people married before October 1906 to a partner from a different island; and people who can show that their lives would be in danger if they went home.","The Prime Minister, Alfred Deakin, says that the new groups can stay. He says the order to deport everyone else will not be cancelled.",{"if":{"cont":"no"},"t":"You think about 1887 and 1888, when you were on your island. You were in Queensland on 31 December 1886, but you have not lived here continuously since then."},"Now you must decide."],"choices":[{"label":"Collect your papers and apply for a certificate of exemption.","to":"end_stay","if":{"cont":"yes"}},{"label":"Collect your papers and apply for a certificate of exemption.","to":"end_stay","if":{"cont":"no","land":"yes"}},{"label":"Collect your papers and apply for a certificate of exemption.","to":"end_deport","if":{"cont":"no","land":"no"}},{"label":"Leave on your own passage rather than wait for an order.","to":"end_early","set":{"left":"late"}},{"label":"Do not apply. Leave the district quietly and hide.","to":"end_hide","if":{"cont":"no","land":"no"}}],"real":[{"paras":["A Queensland Royal Commission reported on 30 June 1906. It advised that more groups should be exempt from deportation. It found that some 4,000 to 4,500 Islanders were liable for deportation. On 7 August 1906 Deakin accepted most of the recommendations, and the amendment received assent on 12 October 1906."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["The new exemption groups were Islanders who had lived continuously in Australia since 31 December 1886, the aged and infirm, those with children educated in state schools, owners of freehold land, those married before October 1906 to a partner not from their own island, and those who could show that their lives would be in danger if returned home. Sources give slightly different dates for the marriage cut-off."],"src":["moore2000","mcneill"]},{"paras":["In 1906 the Pacific Islanders’ Association met regularly in Mackay, hired a solicitor, set by-laws and formed branches at Proserpine and Bowen. Henry Tongoa and Alick Mallicoola travelled to Melbourne and arrived on 13 September 1906. They met Prime Minister Deakin on 17 September and 1 October and gave him a petition from 427 members. Deakin said that the deportation provision would not be reversed."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["The Association was not united. Rockhampton Islanders saw themselves as separate from the Mackay group, and New Hebrideans and Solomon Islanders often did not work together. Moore says that the Islanders’ leaders were able to work in both Melanesian and Australian society."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["To encourage the change to white labour, the Commonwealth paid about £1 to £2 a ton of sugar to cane farmers and millers who used only white labour. In 1902 this was an excise and a rebate. In 1903 it became an excise and a bounty, and the bounty rose to £3 in 1905. By 1905, 78 per cent of farmers and labour in the sugar industry were white, and by 1911 it was 94 per cent."],"src":["moore2000","mcneill"]}]},"end_stay":{"id":"end_stay","title":"You stay","text":["You gather your papers. They include your agreement papers and a statement from a farmer who knows you. You apply for a certificate of exemption. A government officer looks at your papers.",{"if":{"cont":"yes"},"t":"The officer sees that you have lived in Queensland continuously since 1884. That means you meet the rule about continuous residence since 31 December 1886."},{"if":{"cont":"no"},"t":"You went home in 1887, so the rule about continuous residence does not help you. But you own your block of land, and owners of freehold land are one of the exempt groups."},{"if":{"land":"yes","cont":"yes"},"t":"You also own your block of land, which the officer notes."},"The certificate is granted. It is a printed paper with your name. It protects you, but it does not protect the people who do not get one.",{"if":{"abo":"partner"},"t":"She is still in the district. Her life is still controlled by the law of 1897, and you both still have to deal with the Protector. You stay in the district where her family lives."},"In 1907 and 1908 many of your friends and neighbours are put on ships. Some of them have lived in Queensland for more than twenty years.","You stay in a country where the laws, and many people, treat you as an outsider. You work, you go to church, and you meet others on Sundays. The community continues."],"real":[{"paras":["The Pacific Island Labourers Act 1901 and its 1906 amendment led to the issue of certificates of exemption. One General Certificate of Exemption, issued to an Islander known as Calleyho, is dated 4 May 1907."],"src":["peo"]},{"paras":["The official number of Islanders allowed to remain was 1,654. Patricia Mercer’s research suggests that about 2,500 remained, so almost 1,000 avoided deportation."],"src":["moore2000","naaSSI"]},{"paras":["Today the descendants of Pacific Islanders brought to Australia are a distinct cultural group, called the Australian South Sea Islander community. Many have shared South Sea Islander and Aboriginal heritage. In 2000 the Queensland Government recognised South Sea Islanders as a distinct cultural group and acknowledged the discrimination and injustice they had experienced."],"src":["naaSSI","nma"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You were allowed to stay","summary":"This ending shows the people who were allowed to stay. The official number was 1,654. Research suggests that about 2,500 Pacific Islanders actually remained. Around 7,500 were forced to leave. QUASSIC says that today’s Australian South Sea Islander population is largely descended from time-expired labourers and ticket-holders who stayed."},"end_deport":{"id":"end_deport","title":"The ship","text":["You gather your papers and apply. The officer asks where you lived on 31 December 1886 and whether you have lived in Australia continuously since then. You tell him that you were in Queensland then, but that you went home in 1887 and came back in 1889. He says that this means you have not lived here continuously.","You have no land. You have no child at a state school and no marriage that counts.",{"if":{"abo":"partner"},"t":"You tell him about her. The officer writes it down. He says that it does not stop the order."},"Your application is refused. In 1907 you are put on a ship with other men, women and children. You are about forty. You have lived in Queensland for most of your adult life.","The ship lands you somewhere in the islands. It may not be the place where you grew up, because the Act did not say that people must be taken to their home villages.","Your village has changed. Some of your family have died. Some of the land is used by others. You speak English and Pidgin well, and your clothes and habits are not the same as those of the people around you.",{"if":{"abo":"partner"},"t":"You and she are on different sides of the sea. You do not know if you will see each other again."}],"real":[{"paras":["The final deportation began in late 1906 and continued until mid-1908. It took longer than the government had planned. The Pacific Island Branch of the Queensland Immigration Department closed on 31 July 1908."],"src":["naaPILA","moore2000"]},{"paras":["The Act did not say that people had to be returned to their home villages. Governments planned to arrange work in the Solomon Islands, Fiji and the New Hebrides, or return to mission stations. The National Archives holds records about the consequences of returning people to the wrong island, and about Islanders who had married into the Aboriginal community."],"src":["moore2000","naaSSI"]},{"paras":["McNeill and Marmo write that Islander men who lived with or had married Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander women were deported anyway in some cases. They write that 17 of the people deported would otherwise have met the criteria to stay, but had married Aboriginal or Torres Strait Islander people."],"src":["mcneill"]},{"paras":["Some people deported in 1906 to 1908 had arrived in Australia at such a young age that they had no memory of their home country."],"src":["nma"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You were deported","summary":"This ending shows the people who were deported. Deportations began in late 1906 and continued until mid-1908. The National Archives says about 7,500 people were forced to leave. One research paper says that estimates of those deported range from 4,500 to 7,000, including children. Some were separated from partners and children who stayed."},"end_early":{"id":"end_early","title":"On your own passage","text":[{"if":{"left":"early"},"t":"In 1902 you decide to go. You take your savings and pay for a passage. You sell what you cannot carry."},{"if":{"left":"late"},"t":"It is 1906. You decide to leave on your own passage rather than wait for an order. You take your savings and pay your fare. You sell what you cannot carry."},{"if":{"abo":"partner"},"t":"You talk with her about it. She says that she will stay in her own country. You each make your own decision. You leave knowing that you may not meet again."},"Your ship goes to the islands. You choose when you pack and what you take. Many other people did not have that choice.","You arrive after many years in Queensland. Your village has changed. You are glad to be there, and you also miss what you built in Queensland."],"real":[{"paras":["The Act was meant to remove Pacific Islanders by limiting new arrivals and encouraging people to leave before the deportations began."],"src":["naaPILA","moore2000"]},{"paras":["In 1901, 839 Islanders returned to the islands and 1,678 arrived. At the end of 1904, after recruiting ended, there were still 7,879 Islanders in Queensland."],"src":["moore2000"]},{"paras":["The 1902 petition argued that some home islands had become German possessions and that tribal lands had been sold, so families might not welcome people back. In 1903 the British Resident Commissioner in the New Hebrides warned that, unless returning labourers wanted plantation work, Australia would have to supervise their return to their villages or buy land for new villages."],"src":["moore2000"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You chose to leave","summary":"This ending shows people who left by their own decision. The 1901 Act limited new arrivals, ended licences and made staying harder, so many Islanders left before the deportations. In 1901, 839 Islanders returned to their islands. They still lost the homes and lives they had made in Queensland."},"end_home":{"id":"end_home","title":"Home","text":["You stay at home in 1889. You work, and you tell your family what Queensland was like.","Years later you hear that the new Australian Commonwealth has passed a law to send most Islanders home. It does not apply to you.","From 1906 to 1908 people who stayed in Queensland come back to the islands on government ships. Some have lived in Queensland for twenty years or more. Some do not find a welcome in the villages they left.",{"if":{"help":"asked"},"t":"You think about the man in the next hut and the hospital. You do not know if he is alive."},{"if":{"help":"stayed"},"t":"You think about the man in the next hut and the nights you sat with him. You do not know if he is alive."},{"if":{"help":"silent"},"t":"You think about the man in the next hut. You do not know if he is alive."},{"if":{"abo":"contact"},"t":"You also think about the creek and the people who fished there. You do not know what happened to them."}],"real":[{"paras":["The labour trade was designed as a circular migration. Most first-indenture labourers were meant to serve three years and go home. Many re-engaged, in Queensland or after returning to the islands."],"src":["quassic6","moore2000"]},{"paras":["More than 14,564 Islander labourers died in Queensland between 1868 and 1906. This is about 24 per cent of the number of indenture contracts."],"src":["quassic6"]},{"paras":["The Queensland State Archives holds records of recruitment, agreements, employment, health, deportation and the Islanders who remained, for 1867 to 1948."],"src":["qsa"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You went home in 1887","summary":"This ending shows the outcome that the labour system was designed for: a three-year agreement followed by a return home. Records show that the number of Islanders who died in Queensland between 1868 and 1906 was equal to about 24 per cent of the number of contracts. Going home early meant avoiding the 1901 law, but it did not undo the harm done to people who were taken or deceived, or to the communities they left."},"end_hide":{"id":"end_hide","title":"Hiding","text":["You do not apply. You leave the plantation district and live quietly on the edge of a town. You take small jobs.",{"if":{"abo":"partner"},"t":"She says she cannot hide you, and she does not want trouble brought to her family. You accept her decision."},"You have no certificate. You cannot sign a work agreement in your own name, and you cannot ask an official for help. You change where you sleep.","In 1907 and 1908 officers come to Islander settlements with lists. You are not found. You do not know how long that will last.","Years pass. The law stays in force. You do not know whether you are safe or whether someone will report you."],"real":[{"paras":["An estimated 900 people hid in the bush to avoid deportation. This estimate is from a 2019 magazine article by Alex McKinnon, cited in a research paper."],"src":["mcneill"]},{"paras":["The official number of Islanders allowed to remain was 1,654. Patricia Mercer’s research suggests the actual number remaining was around 2,500, which suggests that almost 1,000 avoided deportation."],"src":["moore2000","naaSSI"]},{"paras":["Under the Act, a person who was suspected of being a Pacific Islander had to prove that they were not."],"src":["naaPILA"]}],"end":true,"kind":"You avoided deportation","summary":"This ending shows people who avoided deportation by hiding. No one knows exactly how many. One researcher’s estimate, cited in a 2023 paper, is that about 900 people hid in the bush. The official number allowed to remain was 1,654, but research suggests that about 2,500 stayed. Harry’s path is an invented example. No record tells his story."}},"behind":{"intro":"This story is fiction built on real history. The real history is given in the panels called “Real history” and is listed with its sources on the Sources page. Everything else is invented, and it is listed here.","sections":[{"kind":"made","h":"What is invented","list":["Harry: his age, his island and language (the story does not name them), how he joined the ship, the name the overseer gives him, and everything he does, thinks and feels. The three ways of joining are based on ways the records describe, but Harry’s story is made up.","The Government Agent, the interpreter, the doctor, the overseer, the plantation and its owner, the hospital, the man in the next hut, the farmers, the officer who hears the application, and the Protector. None of them is a real person, and none has a name.","The man at the creek and the woman from his family. They are invented. The story gives them no names, nations or customs, because inventing these would be wrong. What they say and decide is invented. The Yuibera and other peoples named in the real history panels are real. The characters are not members of any named people.","The plantation near Mackay, the creek, the farm next door and the mill that Harry works near later. Real places such as Mackay, the Pioneer River, Bundaberg, the Burdekin and Cairns appear only in the real history panels.","All the conversations, such as the Government Agent’s questions, the Protector’s visit and the officer’s questions at the hearing.","Harry’s dates: arriving in Mackay in 1884, finishing his first agreement in 1887, going home, and returning in 1889. They are made up to fit the real laws and events.","The scenes where Harry decides whether to go home, sign again, buy land, join the Association, hide or leave. These are shaped by what the records show, but no record is known of these particular acts by one person.","The ending scenes: the hearing, the ship, the return to the village, and the hiding. They are based on the real exemption rules, deportation records and estimates, but the details are invented.","Real people, such as the leaders of the Pacific Islanders’ Association, appear only in the real history panels. They are not characters in the story."]},{"kind":"plain","h":"What the story leaves out on purpose","list":["The story does not describe the killing of Aboriginal people, or the violence on recruiting ships, in detail. The panels mention these things briefly because they are part of the history.","The story is about the Mackay district, with short notes on Bundaberg, the Burdekin and Cairns. It does not cover the Torres Strait, other industries where Islanders worked, the Chinese, Japanese, Javanese and other workers in the sugar districts, or the lives of Islander women and children in detail.","It does not show the islands in detail, because the people there were many different peoples, and the author has no right to speak for them.","It ends in 1907 and 1908. It does not cover what happened later to the people who stayed, apart from short notes in the panels.","It uses “Islander” and “Pacific Islander” as the main terms. “Australian South Sea Islander” is the term used today for descendants. The community may have other preferred terms."]},{"kind":"plain","h":"Fairness to the people in the story","list":["People in this story make choices. In real history, Pacific Islanders chose, argued, petitioned, organised, saved, bought land, raised families and kept their communities together. The story tries to show this and not only show people as victims.","The word “Kanaka” is in the records and is offensive today. The story does not use it. “Blackbirding” is often used for the whole labour trade. Historians separate kidnapping from other kinds of recruiting. The story shows all of them.","The story does not decide whether the system should be called slavery. QUASSIC explains that indenture was not legally slavery. It also says that Australian South Sea Islanders call themselves descendants of slaves. Both are given in the real history panels.","Aboriginal people appear as people with their own business. They are not helpers or guides for Harry.","Some source documents use words and attitudes that are offensive today. The story quotes very few of them, and only where needed."]}]},"discuss":[{"h":"Discuss","qs":[{"q":"Why did Queensland planters want labour from the Pacific Islands, and why did the government allow it?"},{"q":"QUASSIC says the labour trade cannot be split simply into kidnapping and free choice. Which parts of the story support this?"},{"q":"Pay was £6 a year, and in the first year of an agreement the death rate was about 81 per 1,000 compared with about 15 per 1,000 for Europeans. What do these numbers tell you, and what do they not tell you?"},{"q":"How did Pacific Islanders respond to the 1901 Act? What does this show about their agency?"},{"q":"How were Aboriginal people and South Sea Islanders treated in similar ways, and in different ways, by Queensland governments?"},{"q":"Harry could stay in one ending and was deported in another. What was the difference, and was the rule fair?"},{"q":"Why is it important to know which parts of the story are invented? How can readers check?"}]},{"h":"Write","qs":[{"q":"Write 200 words as an Islander in Mackay in 1906. Explain to the Royal Commission why you should be allowed to stay. Use only facts from the real history panels."},{"q":"Choose one ending. Write a paragraph that explains what the ending tells us about the real history, using at least two real history panels."},{"q":"Research task: find one source in Queensland State Archives, the National Archives of Australia or Trove about South Sea Islanders in your region. Write down what it says, who wrote it and whose voice is missing. Some records are distressing, so talk to your teacher first."}]}],"openingIntro":["You are Harry, a young man from an island in the New Hebrides. In 1884 you are about seventeen. A ship comes to your island, and the choices you make from then on take you to the sugar fields of the Mackay district in Queensland, and through the years to the new law of 1901.","You will make choices. Each choice has a cost. There are five endings, and none of them is simple. Some paths end with you leaving by your own choice, one with you staying, and some with you being ordered to go. Try more than one path.","Harry is an invented character. Behind the Story explains what is real and what is invented."]}}};
